// Sections 87-104: control flow.

render_section_87 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\A statement is the unit a procedure body is built from. Most statement
        \\forms finish by consuming a single `;` if one is there and moving on if
        \\it is not. A semicolon is therefore a terminator the parser tolerates,
        \\not a separator it demands: a statement ends where its grammar ends, and
        \\the terminator carries no information the parser did not already have.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a := 1
        \\    b := 2;
        \\    c := a + b
        \\    print("a=% b=% c=%\n", a, b, c)
        \\}
    );
    code_block(2, "text", #string
        \\a=1 b=2 c=3
    );
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\The forms that offer the optional semicolon are the ones that end in an
        \\expression or a short clause: declarations, assignments, expression
        \\statements, `return`, `break`, `continue`, `using`, and the directive
        \\statements. A form that ends in a body does not offer it, because the
        \\closing brace has already ended the statement and there is nothing left
        \\for the semicolon to terminate. `if n > 0 { ... };`,
        \\`while i < 1 { ... };` and `{ ... };` are each
        \\`error: unexpected token ';'`.
    );
    subheading(4, "Newlines are not terminators");
    paragraph(5, #string,oneline
        \\A line break is ordinary whitespace. Glosso is C-like on this point and
        \\not Go-like: nothing is inserted at the end of a line, so a statement
        \\continues across as many lines as its grammar needs. The consequence is
        \\that a token which could continue the previous expression will continue
        \\it, however the source is laid out.
    );
    code_block(6, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\double :: (n: int) -> int { return n * 2; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    z := double
        \\    (3)
        \\    print("z=%\n", z)
        \\}
    );
    code_block(7, "text", #string
        \\z=6
    );
    note(8, #string,oneline
        \\`double` on one line and `(3)` on the next is one call expression,
        \\`double(3)`, not a reference to `double` followed by a parenthesised
        \\`3`. A leading `(`, `[`, `.` or binary operator on a continuation line
        \\joins the line above it. Terminate the statement with `;` whenever the
        \\next line starts with one of those.
    );
    subheading(9, "Where a semicolon is mandatory");
    paragraph(10, #string,oneline
        \\There are exactly three places where the parser calls for a semicolon
        \\rather than merely accepting one. All three are positions where a
        \\construct is followed by more source that could otherwise be read as a
        \\continuation of it.
    );
    table_header(11, "Place   —   Form");
    table_row(12, "After a switch case label   |   `case 1;`, `case .Red;`, `case P if guard;`");
    table_row(13, "After a switch `else`   |   `else;`");
    table_row(14, "After a label statement   |   `'name;`; the older `'name:` is also accepted");
    paragraph(15, #string,oneline
        \\A case label is an ordinary expression, so without the semicolon the
        \\parser has no way to know that the label has ended and the arm body has
        \\begun. Omitting it reports the first token of the body.
    );
    code_block(16, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    n := 1;
        \\    if n == {
        \\    case 1 print("one\n");
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(17, "text", #string
        \\error: unexpected token 'print'
        \\  --> switch.glo:6:12
        \\  |
        \\6 |     case 1 print("one\n");
        \\  |            ^^^^^
    );
    paragraph(18, #string,oneline
        \\`else` without its semicolon reports the same way: `else print("other");`
        \\is `error: unexpected token 'print'`. The switch shape is set out in
        \\Switching on a Value.
    );
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\The label statement is the one case that is not diagnosed. A `'name`
        \\token is read as a label statement only when the next token is `;` or
        \\`:`; otherwise it is an ordinary expression of type `label`, evaluated
        \\and discarded. The label is then never defined, and the error surfaces
        \\at the jump instead.
    );
    code_block(20, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Jump";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    i := 0;
        \\'here
        \\    i += 1;
        \\    if i < 3 goto('here);
        \\    print("i=%\n", i);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(21, "text", #string
        \\error: no label 'here' in this procedure (define it with `'here:`)
        \\  --> label.glo:8:14
        \\  |
        \\8 |     if i < 3 goto('here);
        \\  |              ^^^^
    );
    note(22, #string,oneline
        \\There is no empty statement. A semicolon is consumed only by the
        \\statement in front of it, so a stray one has nothing to attach to:
        \\both `;` on its own and the second `;` of `a := 1;;` are
        \\`error: unexpected token ';'`.
    );
    subheading(23, "The statement inventory");
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\The parser tests the statement forms in a fixed order and the first
        \\match wins. The order matters only where two forms can begin with the
        \\same token: a nested declaration is recognised before an expression
        \\statement because `name ::` would otherwise start an expression, and a
        \\label is recognised before an expression for the same reason.
    );
    table_header(25, "Form   —   What it is");
    table_row(26, "`#memory ...`   |   A memory-contract overlay.");
    table_row(27, "`name :: (...)`, `name :: struct`, `name :: enum`, `name :: union`, `name :: distinct`   |   A nested declaration, hoisted to file scope.");
    table_row(28, "`{ ... }`   |   A bare block, which introduces a scope.");
    table_row(29, "`'name;`   |   A label.");
    table_row(30, "`return`, `return e`, `return a, b`   |   Leaves the procedure.");
    table_row(31, "`break`, `continue`   |   Leaves or restarts the innermost loop.");
    table_row(32, "`while cond body`, `for ... body`   |   The two loops.");
    table_row(33, "`#inline while ...`, `#inline for ...`   |   The unrolled loops.");
    table_row(34, "`defer stmt`, `#try defer stmt`   |   Runs a statement at block exit.");
    table_row(35, "`using name;`   |   Opens an aggregate's fields into the scope.");
    table_row(36, "`if ...`   |   The conditional, the switch, and the pattern switch.");
    table_row(37, "`#if ...`   |   The static conditional and the static switch.");
    table_row(38, "`#push_context [e] body`, `#push_allocator (e);`   |   Overrides the implicit context or its allocator.");
    table_row(39, "`#comptime e`, `#comptime { ... }`, `#comptime -> T { ... }`   |   Compile-time evaluation.");
    table_row(40, "`#assert cond, msg;`, `#compile_error msg;`   |   Compile-time checks.");
    table_row(41, "`#insert e;`, `#bytes e;`, `#asm ...`   |   Splices a generated statement, raw bytes, or assembly.");
    table_row(42, "`case label;`   |   A case fragment. Legal only inside a `#code` quote.");
    table_row(43, "`x := e`, `x : T = e`, `x :: e`, `a, b := f()`   |   A variable or constant declaration.");
    table_row(44, "`target = value`, `target op= value`   |   Assignment and update assignment.");
    table_row(45, "any expression   |   An expression statement.");
    bullet(46, #string,oneline
        \\A nested `X :: (...)`, `struct`, `enum`, `union` or `distinct` inside a
        \\procedure body is not a local: it is lifted onto the file's declaration
        \\list at parse time and is visible from every procedure in the file.
        \\Blocks, Scope, and Shadowing covers what this does and does not scope.
    );
    bullet(47, #string,oneline
        \\`return`, `break` and `continue` are also expressions, of the bottom
        \\type `#noreturn`, so they may appear where a value is expected. `ifx`
        \\Expressions shows the arms this makes possible.
    );
    bullet(48, #string,oneline
        \\`#pattern P := subject else { ... };` is parsed as an expression and
        \\reaches statement position inside an expression statement. It is
        \\described in Bind or Diverge.
    );
    paragraph(49, #string,oneline
        \\One program that exercises the less common entries, all of which are
        \\statements and none of which need a semicolon that is not shown:
    );
    code_block(50, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Memory";
        \\
        \\Point :: struct { x: int; y: int; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p: Point = .{ .x = 1, .y = 2 };
        \\    using p;
        \\    print("using % %\n", x, y);
        \\
        \\    { inner := 3; print("block %\n", inner); }
        \\
        \\    #assert 1 + 1 == 2, "arithmetic still works";
        \\    N :: #comptime -> int { total := 0; for i: 1..=4 total += i; return total; };
        \\    print("N %\n", N);
        \\
        \\    #insert "generated := 5;";
        \\    print("generated %\n", generated);
        \\
        \\    defer print("deferred\n");
        \\
        \\    #push_allocator(temp_allocator()) { print("push_allocator\n"); }
        \\    ctx := context;
        \\    #push_context ctx { print("push_context\n"); }
        \\
        \\    #if OUT.os == Target_OS.Windows print("static if\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(51, "text", #string
        \\using 1 2
        \\block 3
        \\N 10
        \\generated 5
        \\push_allocator
        \\push_context
        \\static if
        \\deferred
    );
}

render_section_88 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\`if` takes a condition and a body. The condition is not parenthesised,
        \\and the body is either a braced block or one statement. Parentheses are
        \\accepted around a condition, but only as grouping; they are not part of
        \\the form. `else` follows the body, and `else if` chains without a
        \\separate keyword.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    n := 5;
        \\
        \\    if n > 3 print("gt3\n");
        \\
        \\    if n > 100 print("gt100\n"); else print("le100\n");
        \\
        \\    if n == 5 {
        \\        print("eq5\n");
        \\    } else if n == 6 {
        \\        print("eq6\n");
        \\    } else {
        \\        print("other\n");
        \\    }
        \\
        \\    if (n > 3) && (n < 10) print("parens are grouping\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(2, "text", #string
        \\gt3
        \\le100
        \\eq5
        \\parens are grouping
    );
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\An unbraced body is a single statement, which includes a nested `if`.
        \\Either spelling gets its own scope, so a declaration made in an
        \\unbraced body is no more visible afterwards than one made in a braced
        \\one: `if n > 0 x := 5;` followed by a use of `x` is
        \\`error: unbound 'x'`.
    );
    subheading(4, "The dangling else");
    paragraph(5, #string,oneline
        \\When an `else` could attach to either of two nested `if` statements, it
        \\attaches to the nearest one. The parser reads the inner `if` as the
        \\outer one's body, and the `else` it then meets belongs to the statement
        \\it is still parsing.
    );
    code_block(6, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    n := 5;
        \\    if n > 0
        \\        if n > 100 print("inner\n");
        \\        else print("dangling else went to the inner if\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(7, "text", #string
        \\dangling else went to the inner if
    );
    note(8, #string,oneline
        \\Indentation has no effect on this. An `else` that is meant for the outer
        \\`if` has to be forced there by bracing the inner one.
    );
    subheading(9, "Truthiness");
    paragraph(10, #string,oneline
        \\A condition does not have to be `bool`. Branching on a value applies a
        \\truthiness rule chosen by the value's representation, and the same rule
        \\serves `if` and `while`. The rule is per representation rather than per
        \\type, which is why it is total over the scalar kinds and undefined for
        \\anything with no distinguished zero.
    );
    table_header(11, "Kind   —   Truthy when");
    table_row(12, "`bool`   |   The value is `true`.");
    table_row(13, "integer, enum   |   The value is not zero. An enum is tested by discriminant, so its first constant is falsy.");
    table_row(14, "float   |   The value compares ordered-unequal to zero, so `0.0`, `-0.0` and NaN are all falsy.");
    table_row(15, "pointer, many-pointer, function pointer, `label`   |   The value is not null.");
    table_row(16, "`?T` whose payload is pointer-shaped   |   The value is present.");
    table_row(17, "`string`, `cstring`, `string16`, `cstring16`   |   `.count` is not zero.");
    table_row(18, "`[]T`, `[..]T`   |   `.count` is not zero.");
    code_block(19, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Level :: enum { Info; Warn; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    if 0     print("0 truthy\n");     else print("0 falsy\n");
        \\    if -2    print("-2 truthy\n");    else print("-2 falsy\n");
        \\    if 0.0   print("0.0 truthy\n");   else print("0.0 falsy\n");
        \\    if ""    print("\"\" truthy\n");  else print("\"\" falsy\n");
        \\    if "x"   print("\"x\" truthy\n"); else print("\"x\" falsy\n");
        \\
        \\    lv := Level.Info;
        \\    if lv    print("Level.Info truthy\n"); else print("Level.Info falsy\n");
        \\
        \\    p: ?*int = .None;
        \\    if p     print("null ?*int truthy\n"); else print("null ?*int falsy\n");
        \\
        \\    xs: [3]int = .[1, 2, 3];
        \\    empty := xs[0..0];
        \\    if empty print("empty view truthy\n"); else print("empty view falsy\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(20, "text", #string
        \\0 falsy
        \\-2 truthy
        \\0.0 falsy
        \\"" falsy
        \\"x" truthy
        \\Level.Info falsy
        \\null ?*int falsy
        \\empty view falsy
    );
    paragraph(21, #string,oneline
        \\`?*T` is truthy exactly when it is present because the optional pointer
        \\is stored in the pointer's null niche: `.None` is the null bit pattern,
        \\so the presence test and the null test are the same instruction. An
        \\optional over a payload with no niche has no such coincidence, and it
        \\has no rule at all.
    );
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\A value whose kind is not in the table is rejected when the branch is
        \\lowered. A struct condition is
        \\`error: values of type P have no native truthiness rule`, and
        \\`o: ?s64` is
        \\`error: values of type Option_int have no native truthiness rule`. Test
        \\a struct against something explicit, and match an optional with
        \\`if #pattern .Some(v) = o` or `if o == { case .Some(v); ... }`.
    );
    subheading(23, "The three conditionals compared");
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\`if` is the only one of the three that accepts a non-`bool` condition.
        \\`ifx` coerces its condition to `bool` because it is an expression whose
        \\arms have to agree on a type, and `#if` demands a `bool` that is also
        \\known at compile time because it selects which branch is checked at all.
    );
    table_header(25, "Construct   —   Condition   —   Evaluated");
    table_row(26, "`if`, `while`   |   Any kind with a truthiness rule   |   Run time");
    table_row(27, "`ifx`   |   Must be `bool`   |   Run time");
    table_row(28, "`#if`   |   Must be `bool` and compile-time known   |   Compile time");
    paragraph(29, #string,oneline
        \\`ifx 3 "a" else "b"` is `error: got int, expected bool`, and `#if 1`
        \\is `error: #if condition must be bool, got comptime_int`. The
        \\expression form is covered in `ifx` Expressions and the static form in
        \\Static Branching with `#if`.
    );
    paragraph(30, #string,oneline
        \\An `if` whose condition is a subject followed by `== {` is not a
        \\conditional at all but a switch, and an `if` whose condition begins with
        \\`#pattern` is a pattern test. Both are separate forms of the same
        \\keyword; see Switching on a Value and Pattern Matching with `#pattern`.
    );
}

render_section_89 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\Glosso has no `switch` keyword. A switch is an `if` whose condition is a
        \\subject expression followed immediately by `==` and `{`. The braces hold
        \\case arms rather than statements, each arm opening with a `case` label
        \\that ends in a semicolon, and the default arm is spelled `else;`.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\classify :: (n: int) -> string {
        \\    if n == {
        \\    case ..=0;     return "non-positive";
        \\    case 1, 2, 3;  return "small";
        \\    case 4..=9;    return "single digit";
        \\    case 10..;     return "large";
        \\    }
        \\    return "unreachable";
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("% % % % %\n", classify(-5), classify(0), classify(2), classify(7), classify(99));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(2, "text", #string
        \\non-positive non-positive small single digit large
    );
    subheading(3, "Why `== {` is a switch");
    paragraph(4, #string,oneline
        \\The rule lives in the binary-operator loop of the expression parser:
        \\`==` stops binding as an operator when the token after it is `{`. It is
        \\a lexical rule rather than an `if`-specific one, and it costs nothing,
        \\because `{` can never begin an expression in Glosso. An aggregate
        \\literal is written `.{ ... }`, so a brace where a right operand was
        \\expected is always a mistake.
    );
    code_block(5, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\P :: struct { a: int; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p: P = { .a = 1 };
        \\    print("%\n", p.a);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(6, "text", #string
        \\error: unexpected token '{'
        \\  --> brace.glo:6:12
        \\  |
        \\6 |     p: P = { .a = 1 };
        \\  |            ^
    );
    paragraph(7, #string,oneline
        \\Only the `==` directly in front of the brace is affected, so an ordinary
        \\comparison followed by a block is unambiguous: `if a == b { ... }` is a
        \\conditional, because the token after `==` is `b`. To switch on the
        \\result of a comparison, parenthesise it and let the outer `==` be the
        \\switch marker: `if (a == b) == { case true; ... }`. Any expression may
        \\be the subject, including a call or an arithmetic expression.
    );
    subheading(8, "The shape");
    code_block(9, "text", #string
        \\if [#partial] <subject> == {
        \\case <label> [, <label>]* ;
        \\    <statements>
        \\...
        \\else;
        \\    <statements>
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(10, #string,oneline
        \\The semicolon after a label and after `else` is mandatory, and it is
        \\mandatory because a label is an ordinary expression: nothing else marks
        \\where the label ends and the arm body begins. Omitting it reports the
        \\first token of the body, `error: unexpected token 'print'` for
        \\`case 1 print("one\n");`. Arm bodies are statement lists, not blocks,
        \\and they are not braced.
    );
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\An arm runs to the next `case`, the `else`, or the closing brace. There
        \\is no implicit fall-through and no `break` is needed to prevent it;
        \\entering the next arm's body has to be asked for with `#falling`, which
        \\is described together with multi-label arms in `case A, B;` Groups and
        \\`#falling`.
    );
    subheading(12, "Case labels by subject type");
    paragraph(13, #string,oneline
        \\A label is a literal, a range, an enum constant, or a union variant,
        \\depending on what the subject is. Several labels may share one arm by
        \\separating them with commas.
    );
    table_header(14, "Subject   —   Label spellings");
    table_row(15, "integer   |   `case 7;`, `case 1, 2, 3;`, `case ..=0;`, `case 4..=9;`, `case 10..;`");
    table_row(16, "float   |   `case 2.5;`");
    table_row(17, #string,oneline
        \\`string`   |   `case "red";`, `case "red", "green";`
    );
    table_row(18, #string,oneline
        \\character   |   `case #char "q";`
    );
    table_row(19, "`bool`   |   `case true;`, `case false;`");
    table_row(20, "enum   |   `case Level.Warn;`, `case .Warn;`, `case .Warn, .Error;`");
    table_row(21, "tagged union   |   `case Name;`, `case .Name;`, `case Name(text);`, `case .Name(text);`");
    table_row(22, "`?T`, `E!T`   |   `case .Some(v);`, `case .None;`, `case .Ok(v);`, `case .Err(e);`");
    table_row(23, "any   |   `else;`");
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\Range labels go through the `ToPattern` conversion rather than through
        \\dedicated grammar, so `4..=9` in a label is the same range expression it
        \\would be anywhere else. Its bounds must be known at compile time. A bare
        \\`..` is not accepted as a label: `case ..;` is
        \\`error: bare '..' needs an index context`. Ranges in Patterns and
        \\`ToPattern` covers the conversion.
    );
    code_block(25, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Level :: enum { Info; Warn; Error; }
        \\Token :: union { Name: string; Code: s64; Eof: void; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    lv := Level.Warn;
        \\    if lv == { case Level.Info; print("qualified Info\n"); case Level.Warn; print("qualified Warn\n"); }
        \\    if lv == { case .Info; print("dotted Info\n"); case .Warn, .Error; print("dotted Warn or Error\n"); }
        \\
        \\    t: Token = .Code(42);
        \\    if t == {
        \\    case Name(text); print("bare Name %\n", text);
        \\    case Code(v);    print("bare Code %\n", v);
        \\    case Eof;        print("bare Eof\n");
        \\    }
        \\
        \\    e: Token = .Eof;
        \\    if e == {
        \\    case .Name(text);   print("dotted Name %\n", text);
        \\    case .Code(v);      print("dotted Code %\n", v);
        \\    case .Eof(nothing); print("dotted Eof, payload is the empty tuple: %\n", nothing == .());
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(26, "text", #string
        \\qualified Warn
        \\dotted Warn or Error
        \\bare Code 42
        \\dotted Eof, payload is the empty tuple: 1
    );
    note(27, #string,oneline
        \\An enum constant may be written qualified or with a leading dot, but a
        \\union variant may not be qualified here: `case Token.Name(s);` is
        \\`error: '' is not a variant of union Token`. Use `Name(s)` or
        \\`.Name(s)`. The qualified spelling is accepted in a `#pattern` switch,
        \\which is a different form.
    );
    subheading(28, "Nothing matched");
    paragraph(29, #string,oneline
        \\When no arm matches and there is no `else;`, control falls out of the
        \\switch and continues after it. There is no trap, because a statement
        \\switch is not obliged to produce anything. The expression form is
        \\obliged to, and behaves differently; see `ifx` Expressions.
    );
    code_block(30, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    n := 7;
        \\    if n == { case 1; print("one\n"); case 2; print("two\n"); }
        \\    print("fell out of the int switch\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(31, "text", #string
        \\fell out of the int switch
    );
    paragraph(32, #string,oneline
        \\Integer, float, string, character, `bool` and enum subjects are not
        \\coverage-checked in this form, and duplicate labels are not diagnosed:
        \\the first matching arm wins and the rest are dead. A tagged-union
        \\subject is the exception. Its switch must name every variant, or carry
        \\`else;`, or be marked `#partial`; otherwise it is
        \\`error: switch is exhaustive by default; add else or #partial`.
    );
    paragraph(33, #string,oneline
        \\`#partial` waives that requirement and is written immediately after
        \\`if`, before the subject and before a `#pattern` marker. Elsewhere it is
        \\rejected: `if #partial true { }` is
        \\`error: #partial can only modify an if-switch`, and
        \\`if #pattern #partial s == { ... }` is
        \\`error: unexpected directive 'partial'`.
    );
    code_block(34, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Token :: union { Name: string; Code: s64; Eof: void; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    t: Token = .Code(7);
        \\    if #partial t == { case .Code(v); print("partial code %\n", v); }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(35, "text", #string
        \\partial code 7
    );
    note(36, #string,oneline
        \\A union payload is reachable only through a capture in the arm that
        \\proved the tag. Reading `t.Code` directly is
        \\`error: tagged-union payload '.Code' may only be bound by a pattern
        \\match`. Reading a Union Payload gives the rule and the escape hatch, and
        \\a raw union has no tag to switch on at all:
        \\`error: cannot switch on a raw union (it has no tag)`.
    );
    paragraph(37, #string,oneline
        \\One arm may not capture from several tags whose payloads differ:
        \\`case .Name(x), .Code(x);` is `error: a case with several tags cannot
        \\capture a payload - the tags have different payload types`. Grouping
        \\tags that share a body is covered in `case A, B;` Groups and
        \\`#falling`, and the coverage analysis itself in Exhaustiveness, Guards,
        \\and Reachability.
    );
}

render_section_90 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\`ifx` is the expression conditional. It exists because `if` is a
        \\statement and produces nothing, and because a value that depends on a
        \\test should not have to be built by declaring a variable and assigning
        \\to it from two branches. Every `ifx` must produce a value on every path,
        \\which is the single rule the rest of its behaviour follows from.
    );
    table_header(1, "Form   —   Shape");
    table_row(2, "conditional   |   `ifx cond then-expr else else-expr`");
    table_row(3, "value switch   |   `ifx subject == { case L; expr; ... else; expr; }`");
    table_row(4, "pattern switch   |   `ifx #pattern [subject] == { case P; expr; ... }`");
    code_block(5, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Color :: enum { Red; Green; Blue; }
        \\Shape :: union { Circle: f64; Rect: f64; Empty: void; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    enabled := true;
        \\    print("%\n", ifx enabled "on" else "off");
        \\
        \\    n := 5;
        \\    print("%\n", ifx n > 10 "big" else ifx n > 3 "mid" else "small");
        \\
        \\    score := 85;
        \\    print("%\n", ifx score == { case 90; "A"; case 85; "B"; else; "C"; });
        \\
        \\    c := Color.Blue;
        \\    print("%\n", ifx c == { case .Red; "red"; case .Green; "green"; case .Blue; "blue"; });
        \\
        \\    s: Shape = .Rect(3.0);
        \\    print("%\n", ifx #pattern s == {
        \\    case Shape.Circle(r); 3.0 * r * r;
        \\    case Shape.Rect(w);   w * w;
        \\    case Shape.Empty;     0.0;
        \\    });
        \\
        \\    print("%\n", ifx #pattern == { case n > 3; "gt"; else; "le"; });
        \\}
    );
    code_block(6, "text", #string
        \\on
        \\mid
        \\B
        \\blue
        \\9
        \\gt
    );
    paragraph(7, #string,oneline
        \\The switch forms share the subject and label grammar of the statement
        \\switch, including the mandatory semicolon after each label and after
        \\`else`. The subject-less pattern form takes arbitrary boolean
        \\predicates as its labels. `ifx` does not accept `#partial`:
        \\`ifx #partial c == { ... }` is
        \\`error: unexpected directive 'partial'`, since waiving coverage would
        \\leave the expression with no value to produce.
    );
    subheading(8, "The `else` is mandatory on the conditional form");
    paragraph(9, #string,oneline
        \\A two-armed test with one arm missing has nothing to yield when the
        \\condition is false, so the omission is an error rather than a `void`
        \\result.
    );
    code_block(10, "text", #string
        \\error: cannot decide a value: an 'ifx' needs an 'else' arm
        \\  --> decide.glo:5:21
        \\  |
        \\5 |     x := ifx b "yes";
        \\  |                     ^
        \\note: add 'else <expr>', or use the switch form 'ifx <subject> == { ... }'
    );
    subheading(11, "Arms end in an expression");
    paragraph(12, #string,oneline
        \\An arm of a switch form is a statement list, so it may compute before it
        \\answers, but its last statement has to be an expression. That expression
        \\is the arm's value.
    );
    code_block(13, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    score := 85;
        \\    w := ifx score == { case 85; print("computing\n"); score * 2; else; 0; };
        \\    print("w %\n", w);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(14, "text", #string
        \\computing
        \\w 170
    );
    paragraph(15, #string,oneline
        \\An arm ending in something that is not an expression is
        \\`error: an ifx arm must end in an expression`, reported at the offending
        \\statement. An arm that ends in a `void` call is subtler: the arm's type
        \\becomes `void`, and the mismatch surfaces on the next arm instead, as
        \\`error: got int, expected void`.
    );
    paragraph(16, #string,oneline
        \\The arms are expressions, not blocks. `ifx b { 1 } else { 2 }` does not
        \\parse: a brace never begins an expression, so this is
        \\`error: unexpected token '{'`.
    );
    subheading(17, "The join across arms");
    paragraph(18, #string,oneline
        \\The arms meet at a numeric join rather than at the first arm's type. An
        \\integer literal beside a float arm becomes the float type, and a sized
        \\integer or a narrower float on one side pulls a literal on the other
        \\side to it. This is the same defaulting the rest of the language uses,
        \\applied to the set of arms.
    );
    code_block(19, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    b := true;
        \\    n: s32 = 4;
        \\    a := ifx b 1 else 2.5;
        \\    c := ifx b n else 7;
        \\    d := ifx b cast(f32)1.0 else 2.0;
        \\    print("% % %\n", type_name(type_of(a)), type_name(type_of(c)), type_name(type_of(d)));
        \\    print("% % %\n", a, c, d);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(20, "text", #string
        \\f64 s32 f32
        \\1 4 1
    );
    paragraph(21, #string,oneline
        \\Arms with no join are an error reported against the arm that does not
        \\fit: `ifx b 1 else "two"` is `error: got int, expected string`.
    );
    subheading(22, "Totality");
    paragraph(23, #string,oneline
        \\A switch form must be provably total or carry `else;`. Totality is
        \\computed, not demanded: an enum or union subject whose arms name every
        \\constructor needs no `else`, which is why the `Color` example above ends
        \\at `case .Blue;`. When the proof fails the compiler names the witnesses
        \\it found.
    );
    code_block(24, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Color :: enum { Red; Green; Blue; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    c := Color.Red;
        \\    name := ifx c == { case .Red; "red"; case .Green; "green"; };
        \\    print("%\n", name);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(25, "text", #string
        \\error: cannot decide a value for this ifx
        \\  --> total.glo:7:13
        \\  |
        \\7 |     name := ifx c == { case .Red; "red"; case .Green; "green"; };
        \\  |             ^^^
        \\note: missing case: Color.Blue
        \\note: cover every case, or add an 'else' arm
    );
    paragraph(26, #string,oneline
        \\Subjects whose domain cannot be enumerated are never total on literals
        \\alone. An `f64` or `string` subject with only literal labels reports
        \\`note: missing case: _`, and the subject-less predicate form reports the
        \\same witness under `error: non-exhaustive #pattern switch`, because an
        \\arbitrary boolean predicate proves nothing about coverage. The analysis
        \\behind the witnesses is described in Exhaustiveness, Guards, and
        \\Reachability.
    );
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\When the subject is known at compile time and no arm matches it, the
        \\failure is escalated rather than left to run time, because the panic
        \\would be unconditional.
    );
    code_block(28, "text", #string
        \\error: no arm matches the comptime value Int(5), and there is no else
        \\  --> known.glo:5:10
        \\  |
        \\5 |     x := ifx N == { case 1; "one"; case 2; "two"; };
        \\  |          ^^^
        \\note: the subject is known at compile time, so this would panic on every evaluation
    );
    paragraph(29, #string,oneline
        \\The lowerer still emits a backstop on the no-arm path, reporting
        \\`ifx matched no arm and has no else` through the assertion machinery.
        \\A correct totality proof makes it unreachable; it exists so that a gap
        \\in the analysis fails loudly rather than yielding an undefined value.
    );
    subheading(30, "Diverging arms");
    paragraph(31, #string,oneline
        \\`return`, `break` and `continue` are expressions of type `#noreturn`,
        \\the bottom type, which coerces to every type because the coercion can
        \\never run. An `ifx` arm may therefore leave instead of producing a
        \\value, which is how a loop body filters and binds in one step.
    );
    code_block(32, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    xs: [5]s64 = .[1, 3, 4, 6, 7];
        \\    total := 0;
        \\    for v: xs {
        \\        w := ifx v < 6 v else break;
        \\        total += w;
        \\    }
        \\    print("total=%\n", total);
        \\
        \\    evens := 0;
        \\    for v: xs {
        \\        w := ifx v % 2 == 0 v else continue;
        \\        evens += w;
        \\    }
        \\    print("evens=%\n", evens);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(33, "text", #string
        \\total=8
        \\evens=10
    );
    note(34, #string,oneline
        \\The join takes the other arm's type first, so a bare numeric literal
        \\beside a diverging arm is checked against `#noreturn` and fails:
        \\`x := ifx b 1 else return -1;` is
        \\`error: got int, expected #noreturn`. Naming the value first
        \\(`ifx b y else return -1`) or writing the `ifx` directly in a `return`
        \\both work, because the expected type is then known from context.
    );
}

render_section_91 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\`#pattern` introduces the pattern language. A pattern is one construct
        \\that does two things at once: it tests a value's shape and it binds
        \\names to the parts it found. That pairing is why patterns are the only
        \\way to read a tagged union's payload, since the binding cannot happen
        \\without the test having succeeded.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\This chapter covers where a pattern may appear and the two rules that
        \\govern all of the positions. The patterns themselves are catalogued in
        \\The Pattern Vocabulary, ranges in Ranges in Patterns and `ToPattern`,
        \\and coverage in Exhaustiveness, Guards, and Reachability.
    );
    table_header(2, "Form   —   Position   —   Meaning");
    table_row(3, "`if #pattern P = subject { ... }`   |   condition   |   One-shot test; captures reach the then-branch.");
    table_row(4, "`if #pattern { P1 = s1; P2 = s2; }`   |   condition   |   Multi-arm test; all arms must match; captures join.");
    table_row(5, "`while #pattern P = subject { ... }`   |   condition   |   Re-tested and re-bound each iteration.");
    table_row(6, "`if [#partial] #pattern [subject] == { case P; ... }`   |   statement   |   Pattern switch.");
    table_row(7, "`ifx #pattern [subject] == { case P; expr; ... }`   |   expression   |   Pattern switch that yields a value.");
    table_row(8, "`#pattern P := subject else { ... };`   |   statement   |   Bind into the enclosing scope or diverge.");
    table_row(9, "`#pattern P`   |   expression   |   A quote: a compile-time value of type `pattern`.");
    paragraph(10, #string,oneline
        \\The parser tells these apart by scanning ahead from the pattern for a
        \\depth-zero `=` or `:=`. A depth-zero `=` makes it a test, a `:=` makes it
        \\the bind-or-diverge form, and neither makes it a quote. `==` is a
        \\different token, so a `== CONST` element inside a pattern is never
        \\mistaken for the test marker, and a `{` found at depth zero ends the
        \\scan because that brace is the enclosing `if`'s body.
    );
    code_block(11, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Shape :: union { Circle: f64; Rect: f64; Empty: void; }
        \\
        \\port_of :: (o: ?s64) -> s64 {
        \\    #pattern .Some(v) := o else { return -1; }
        \\    return v;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    s: Shape = .Circle(2.0);
        \\
        \\    if #pattern Shape.Circle(r) = s  print("test: r = %\n", r);
        \\
        \\    if #pattern Shape.Rect(w) = s { print("rect %\n", w); }
        \\    else                          { print("test with else: not a rect\n"); }
        \\
        \\    o: ?s64 = .Some(3);
        \\    while #pattern .Some(v) = o {
        \\        print("loop: v = %\n", v);
        \\        if v <= 1 { o = .None; } else { o = .Some(v - 1); }
        \\    }
        \\
        \\    if #pattern s == {
        \\    case Shape.Circle(r); print("switch: circle %\n", r);
        \\    case Shape.Rect(w);   print("switch: rect %\n", w);
        \\    case Shape.Empty;     print("switch: empty\n");
        \\    }
        \\
        \\    print("bind or diverge: % %\n", port_of(.Some(8)), port_of(.None));
        \\
        \\    CIRCLE :: #pattern Shape.Circle(r);
        \\    if #pattern #insert CIRCLE = s  print("quote: r = %\n", r);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(12, "text", #string
        \\test: r = 2
        \\test with else: not a rect
        \\loop: v = 3
        \\loop: v = 2
        \\loop: v = 1
        \\switch: circle 2
        \\bind or diverge: 8 -1
        \\quote: r = 2
    );
    paragraph(13, #string,oneline
        \\The multi-arm block form tests several subjects at once and succeeds
        \\only when every arm matches; the captures of all arms are then in scope
        \\together, and a later arm may read an earlier arm's capture.
    );
    code_block(14, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Pair :: struct { first: s64; second: s64; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p: Pair = .{ .first = 4, .second = 9 };
        \\    q: Pair = .{ .first = 4, .second = 1 };
        \\    if #pattern { Pair.{ .first = a, ... } = p; Pair.{ .first = == a, ... } = q; }
        \\        print("both start at %\n", a);
        \\    else
        \\        print("they differ\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(15, "text", #string
        \\both start at 4
    );
    paragraph(16, #string,oneline
        \\A pattern test is an expression of type `bool`, and the subject side is
        \\parsed at a precedence above `&&` and `||`, so
        \\`if #pattern P = subject && g` reads as `(#pattern P = subject) && g`
        \\rather than testing `P` against `subject && g`. The extra condition then
        \\sees the captures.
    );
    code_block(17, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Shape :: union { Circle: f64; Rect: f64; Empty: void; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    s: Shape = .Rect(3.0);
        \\    if #pattern .Rect(w) = s && w > 1.0   print("wide rect %\n", w);
        \\    if #pattern .Rect(w) = s && w > 100.0 print("huge\n"); else print("not huge\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(18, "text", #string
        \\wide rect 3
        \\not huge
    );
    note(19, #string,oneline
        \\Do not put `||` between two pattern tests. It compiles, but `||` short
        \\circuits, so the untaken alternative's captures are in scope and never
        \\written; the arm reads uninitialised storage. `|` inside a single
        \\pattern is the supported spelling for alternatives, and it forces every
        \\alternative to bind the same names at the same types.
    );
    paragraph(20, #string,oneline
        \\A test cannot be negated. `!(#pattern P = subject)` is
        \\`error: a '#pattern' test cannot be negated`, with
        \\`note: its captures would have no scope to bind into - '!' inverts the
        \\value, not the branch they belong to` and a pointer to the `:=` form.
        \\Bind or Diverge covers that form.
    );
    subheading(21, "The quote");
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\`#pattern P` with no subject is not a test but a quote: a value of the
        \\built-in type `pattern`. The type is compile-time only and has no
        \\storage, so a quote is bound with `::` and spliced back into pattern
        \\position with `#insert`. Binding one with `:=` is
        \\`error: a 'pattern' value is compile-time only; bind it with '::' rather
        \\than ':='`. Patterns as Compile-Time Values covers the introspection
        \\intrinsics built on this.
    );
    subheading(23, "Tag-first lowering");
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\A variant pattern lowers to a tag comparison and a branch to the failure
        \\label. The payload is only extracted after that branch, so a pattern
        \\that does not match never forms the payload it was going to bind. The
        \\reason is that a union's storage read as the wrong variant is not a
        \\value of that variant's type at all; forming it first and testing
        \\afterwards would mean building an ill-typed value on every miss.
    );
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\The consequence a program can observe is that a subpattern under a
        \\variant is only ever applied to that variant's payload. An interval test
        \\nested inside `Value.Number(...)` never runs against a `Value.Text`
        \\payload, even though the two share storage.
    );
    code_block(26, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Value :: union { Number: s64; Text: string; Nothing: void; }
        \\
        \\describe :: (v: Value) -> string {
        \\    if #pattern Value.Number(0..=9) = v  return "small number";
        \\    if #pattern Value.Number(n) = v      return "big number";
        \\    if #pattern Value.Text(t) = v        return t;
        \\    return "nothing";
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("%\n", describe(Value.Number(3)));
        \\    print("%\n", describe(Value.Number(400)));
        \\    print("%\n", describe(Value.Text("hello")));
        \\    print("%\n", describe(Value.Nothing()));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(27, "text", #string
        \\small number
        \\big number
        \\hello
        \\nothing
    );
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\The same ordering discipline runs through the other composite patterns.
        \\A slice pattern emits its length test before any index is formed, so an
        \\element is never read out of a slice too short to hold it, and a struct
        \\pattern's fields are tested left to right with each failure jumping
        \\straight to the pattern's failure label.
    );
    paragraph(29, #string,oneline
        \\This is also the mechanism behind the rule that a tagged-union payload
        \\may only be reached through a capture. A direct `value.Variant` read has
        \\no tag test in front of it, so there is nothing to make it well typed:
        \\`error: tagged-union payload '.Code' may only be bound by a pattern
        \\match`. Reading a Union Payload has the full rule.
    );
    subheading(30, "Captures bind in the block that proved the match");
    paragraph(31, #string,oneline
        \\A capture is written where the whole pattern succeeded, not at the
        \\textual point of the binder. A binder that appears early in a pattern
        \\therefore has no effect if a later part of the same pattern rejects, and
        \\an outer name of the same spelling survives untouched.
    );
    code_block(32, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Pair :: struct { first: s64; second: s64; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    d := 7;
        \\    p: Pair = .{ .first = 5, .second = 9 };
        \\    if #pattern Pair.{ .first = d, .second = == 0 } = p { print("matched, d = %\n", d); }
        \\    else                                                { print("rejected, d = %\n", d); }
        \\    print("after, d = %\n", d);
        \\
        \\    q: Pair = .{ .first = 4, .second = 4 };
        \\    if #pattern Pair.{ .first = a, .second = == a } = q  print("both fields are %\n", a);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(33, "text", #string
        \\rejected, d = 7
        \\after, d = 7
        \\both fields are 4
    );
    paragraph(34, #string,oneline
        \\The second half of that program shows the compensating rule: within one
        \\pattern, a later element may refer to an earlier capture, because the
        \\register holding the matched part is computed on the straight-line path
        \\before any sibling test runs. What is deferred is the binding the
        \\surrounding block sees, not the value the pattern is working with.
    );
    bullet(35, #string,oneline
        \\Captures do not escape the construct that proved them. Using a capture
        \\after the `if` that bound it is `error: unbound 'r'`.
    );
    bullet(36, #string,oneline
        \\Alternatives inside one pattern funnel their captures into a shared set
        \\of registers, so an arm body reads the alternative that actually
        \\matched rather than the last one written.
    );
    bullet(37, #string,oneline
        \\A `void` payload still binds a name; the name has a type and no storage,
        \\and it compares equal to the empty tuple `.()`.
    );
    bullet(38, #string,oneline
        \\`#pattern P := subject else { ... };` is the exception that binds into
        \\the enclosing scope. It is sound only because the `else` is required to
        \\diverge, so the binding is unreachable on the failing path.
    );
}

render_section_92 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\A pattern is a shape a value is tested against, together with the names
        \\the test introduces when it succeeds. Every shape below composes with
        \\every other to any depth: a variant capture may hold a struct pattern
        \\whose field pattern is a range, and an or-pattern may join two of those.
        \\The positions in which a pattern may be written are described in the
        \\chapter on pattern matching with `#pattern`; this chapter is the
        \\vocabulary those positions accept.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\Two rules govern how a pattern is read. A bare identifier always
        \\binds, so `LIMIT` in pattern position names the matched value rather
        \\than comparing against the constant `LIMIT`; the `==` form exists for the
        \\comparison. And an element that is not pattern syntax is an ordinary
        \\expression, evaluated at compile time and converted to a pattern, which
        \\is how ranges and spliced quotes enter without new grammar.
    );
    table_header(2, "Shape   —   Spelling   —   Matches");
    table_row(3, "literal   |   `3`, `2.5`, `\"hi\"`, `true`   |   a value equal to it");
    table_row(4, "binding   |   `name`   |   anything; names the matched value");
    table_row(5, "wildcard   |   `_`   |   anything; binds nothing");
    table_row(6, "pointer binding   |   `*name`   |   anything; names a pointer to the place");
    table_row(7, "enum constant   |   `Color.Green`, `.Green`   |   that discriminant");
    table_row(8, "variant, tag only   |   `Shape.Circle`, `.Circle`   |   that tag, payload discarded");
    table_row(9, "variant capture   |   `.Circle(r)`   |   that tag; `r` names the payload");
    table_row(10, "struct, named   |   `Pair.{ .first = a, .second = b }`   |   every field pattern matching");
    table_row(11, "struct, shorthand   |   `Pair.{ .second, ... }`   |   binds `second`, ignores the rest");
    table_row(12, "struct, positional   |   `.{ a, b }`   |   fields in declaration order");
    table_row(13, "slice   |   `.[head, ..., tail]`   |   an array, view or dynamic array of a matching length");
    table_row(14, "or-pattern   |   `P | Q`   |   either alternative");
    table_row(15, "`@` binding   |   `whole @ P`   |   `P`, and names the whole value too");
    table_row(16, "constant   |   `== LIMIT`   |   a value equal to the constant");
    table_row(17, "range   |   `0..=9`, `100..`   |   an integer or enum in the interval");
    subheading(18, "Literals, bindings, and the wildcard");
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\An integer, float, string, `#char` or boolean literal matches a value
        \\equal to it. A bare identifier matches anything and binds it. `_` matches
        \\anything and binds nothing, which is what it is for: a position that must
        \\be present but is not read. `*name` also matches anything, but binds a
        \\pointer to the place the value occupies rather than a copy of it, so an
        \\assignment through it writes back into the subject.
    );
    code_block(20, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Pair :: struct { first: s64; second: s64; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    n := 3; f := 2.5; s := "hi"; b := true;
        \\    if #pattern 3 = n     print("int literal\n");
        \\    if #pattern 2.5 = f   print("float literal\n");
        \\    if #pattern "hi" = s  print("string literal\n");
        \\    if #pattern true = b  print("bool literal\n");
        \\
        \\    if #pattern v = n  print("binding v = %\n", v);
        \\    if #pattern _ = n  print("wildcard\n");
        \\
        \\    p: Pair = .{ .first = 1, .second = 2 };
        \\    if #pattern Pair.{ .first = *fp, ... } = p  fp.* = 42;
        \\    print("through the pointer, first = %\n", p.first);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(21, "text", #string
        \\int literal
        \\float literal
        \\string literal
        \\bool literal
        \\binding v = 3
        \\wildcard
        \\through the pointer, first = 42
    );
    subheading(22, "Variants and enum constants");
    paragraph(23, #string,oneline
        \\A tagged-union variant is written qualified (`Token.Name`) or with a
        \\leading dot (`.Name`); both spellings are accepted everywhere a pattern
        \\is. Parentheses after the tag hold the payload pattern, which is usually
        \\a binding. An enum constant takes the same two spellings and never
        \\carries a payload. A variant written without parentheses proves the tag
        \\and discards the payload.
    );
    code_block(24, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Token :: union { Number: f64; Name: string; Eof: void; }
        \\Color :: enum { Red; Green; Blue; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    t: Token = .Name("total");
        \\    if #pattern Token.Name(text) = t  print("qualified: %\n", text);
        \\    if #pattern .Name(text) = t       print("leading dot: %\n", text);
        \\
        \\    e: Token = .Eof;
        \\    if #pattern Token.Eof = e            print("tag only\n");
        \\    if #pattern Token.Eof(nothing) = e   print("void capture: %\n", nothing == .());
        \\
        \\    c := Color.Green;
        \\    if #pattern Color.Green = c  print("enum qualified\n");
        \\    if #pattern .Green = c       print("enum leading dot\n");
        \\    if #pattern .Blue = c        print("unreached\n"); else print("enum mismatch\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(25, "text", #string
        \\qualified: total
        \\leading dot: total
        \\tag only
        \\void capture: 1
        \\enum qualified
        \\enum leading dot
        \\enum mismatch
    );
    paragraph(26, #string,oneline
        \\A `void` payload still admits a capture: `Token.Eof(nothing)` binds the
        \\empty tuple, so `nothing == .()` is true. `?T` and `E!T` are ordinary
        \\tagged unions to the checker, so `.Some`, `.None`, `.Ok` and `.Err` are
        \\variant patterns like any other; the two types have their own chapters.
    );
    subheading(27, "Struct and slice patterns");
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\A struct pattern is written with the aggregate-literal punctuation:
        \\`Pair.{ ... }` names the type, `.{ ... }` infers it. Fields may be given
        \\by name (`.first = a`), by shorthand (`.second`, which binds a name equal
        \\to the field name), or positionally in declaration order. `...` as the
        \\last element states that the fields not mentioned are ignored; it is
        \\documentation rather than a requirement, because an unmentioned field is
        \\treated as a wildcard in any case, both when matching and in the coverage
        \\analysis. A positional pattern may likewise stop short and bind a prefix
        \\of the fields. A slice pattern uses `.[ ... ]` and matches a fixed array,
        \\a view or a dynamic array; `...` inside it stands for any number of
        \\skipped elements and may appear at most once, so the compiler can fix
        \\each named element's offset from one end or the other.
    );
    code_block(29, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Pair :: struct { first: s64; second: s64; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p: Pair = .{ .first = 4, .second = 9 };
        \\    if #pattern Pair.{ .first = a, .second = b } = p  print("named % %\n", a, b);
        \\    if #pattern Pair.{ .second, ... } = p             print("shorthand %\n", second);
        \\    if #pattern .{ x, y } = p                         print("positional % %\n", x, y);
        \\
        \\    items: [5]s64 = .[10, 20, 30, 40, 50];
        \\    if #pattern .[head, ..., tail] = items[..]  print("head % tail %\n", head, tail);
        \\    if #pattern .[a2, b2, ...] = items[..]      print("prefix % %\n", a2, b2);
        \\    two: [2]s64 = .[7, 8];
        \\    if #pattern .[u, v] = two[..]  print("exact % %\n", u, v);
        \\    if #pattern .[u2] = two[..]    print("unreached\n"); else print("length mismatch\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(30, "text", #string
        \\named 4 9
        \\shorthand 9
        \\positional 4 9
        \\head 10 tail 50
        \\prefix 10 20
        \\exact 7 8
        \\length mismatch
    );
    paragraph(31, #string,oneline
        \\A second `...` is rejected: `.[a, ..., b, ..., c]` is
        \\`error: a slice pattern may use '...' at most once`, because two gaps of
        \\unknown width leave the elements between them at no fixed offset. A slice
        \\pattern applied to a non-array is
        \\`error: slice pattern needs an array or slice value, got int`, and a
        \\struct pattern applied to a non-struct is
        \\`error: struct pattern needs a struct value, got int`.
    );
    subheading(32, "Or-patterns, `@`, and constant patterns");
    paragraph(33, #string,oneline
        \\`P | Q` matches when either alternative matches, and the alternatives
        \\must bind the same names at the same types, because the block that runs
        \\afterwards cannot know which alternative proved the match. `whole @ P`
        \\matches `P` and additionally binds `whole` to the value as a whole, which
        \\is how a pattern can destructure and still keep the original. `== expr`
        \\compares the matched value against `expr` instead of binding; `expr` is an
        \\ordinary expression, so it may be a named constant or an earlier capture
        \\from the same pattern.
    );
    code_block(34, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Shape :: union { Circle: f64; Rect: f64; Empty: void; }
        \\Pair  :: struct { first: s64; second: s64; }
        \\LIMIT :: 255;
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    s: Shape = .Rect(3.0);
        \\    if #pattern Shape.Circle(r) | Shape.Rect(r) = s  print("or-pattern r = %\n", r);
        \\
        \\    q: Pair = .{ .first = 255, .second = 1 };
        \\    if #pattern Pair.{ .first = == LIMIT, ... } = q  print("first is at LIMIT\n");
        \\    if #pattern Pair.{ .first = a, .second = == a } = q  print("fields agree\n");
        \\    else print("fields differ\n");
        \\
        \\    if #pattern whole @ Pair.{ .first, ... } = q
        \\        print("first = %  whole.second = %\n", first, whole.second);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(35, "text", #string
        \\or-pattern r = 3
        \\first is at LIMIT
        \\fields differ
        \\first = 255  whole.second = 1
    );
    paragraph(36, #string,oneline
        \\The three ways an or-pattern's alternatives can disagree each have their
        \\own diagnostic:
        \\`error: or-pattern binds 'r' as f64 here but as int in another alternative`,
        \\`error: or-pattern alternative binds 'q', which the other alternatives do not`,
        \\and `error: or-pattern alternative does not bind 'r', which another
        \\alternative does`.
    );
    subheading(37, "Guards and compound conditions");
    paragraph(38, #string,oneline
        \\A case in a `#pattern` switch may carry a guard: `case P if <cond>;`
        \\matches when `P` matches and `cond` is then true, and a failing guard
        \\tries the next case rather than leaving the switch. That is the
        \\behaviour an `if` nested inside the arm body cannot express. The guard is
        \\checked with the full condition grammar, so it sees the arm's captures
        \\and may itself contain a `#pattern` test that binds more names. Outside a
        \\switch, `&&` composes a test with an ordinary condition in the same way.
    );
    code_block(39, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Shape :: union { Circle: f64; Rect: f64; Empty: void; }
        \\Size  :: union { Big: f64; Small: void; }
        \\
        \\classify :: (r: f64) -> Size {
        \\    if r > 10.0 return Size.Big(r);
        \\    return Size.Small();
        \\}
        \\
        \\describe :: (s: Shape) -> string {
        \\    if #pattern s == {
        \\    case Shape.Circle(r) if r > 100.0;                              return "huge circle";
        \\    case Shape.Circle(r) if #pattern Size.Big(scale) = classify(r);
        \\        print("  scale %\n", scale);
        \\        return "big circle";
        \\    case Shape.Circle(r);  return "small circle";
        \\    case Shape.Rect(w);    return "rect";
        \\    case Shape.Empty;      return "empty";
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("%\n", describe(Shape.Circle(500.0)));
        \\    print("%\n", describe(Shape.Circle(50.0)));
        \\    print("%\n", describe(Shape.Circle(1.0)));
        \\    r: Shape = .Rect(3.0);
        \\    print("%\n", describe(r));
        \\    if #pattern .Rect(w) = r && w > 1.0  print("wide rect %\n", w);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(40, "text", #string
        \\huge circle
        \\  scale 50
        \\big circle
        \\small circle
        \\rect
        \\wide rect 3
    );
    paragraph(41, #string,oneline
        \\A non-bool guard is `error: case guard must be bool, got f64`. A guarded
        \\arm contributes nothing to the coverage analysis, since the guard can
        \\fail at run time; the chapter on exhaustiveness, guards, and reachability
        \\has the consequences.
    );
    note(42, #string,oneline
        \\`||` between two pattern tests compiles but is unsound. `||`
        \\short-circuits, so the captures of the alternative that was not evaluated
        \\are in scope and were never written:
        \\`if #pattern .X(p) = u || #pattern .Y(q) = w print("% %\n", p, q);` prints
        \\`1 0`, where the `0` is an uninitialised capture. Use a switch or nested
        \\tests instead.
    );
    note(43, #string,oneline
        \\A pattern test cannot be negated. `if !(#pattern .Some(v) = o)` is
        \\`error: a '#pattern' test cannot be negated`, with
        \\`note: its captures would have no scope to bind into - '!' inverts the
        \\value, not the branch they belong to` and a second note pointing at the
        \\bind-or-diverge form, which is the construct for leaving on failure.
    );
}

render_section_93 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\`..` and `..=` are operators, not pattern syntax. A case or pattern
        \\element that uses one is therefore parsed as an ordinary expression and
        \\converted into a pattern afterwards, through the `ToPattern` typeclass.
        \\That is why `0..=9`, `100..` and `..` work in pattern position without a
        \\single new production, and why the same conversion admits user types.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\The consequence to keep in mind is that a bound is an expression, so a
        \\bare name in a bound is a constant to look up, not a capture to
        \\introduce. This is the one place inside a pattern where an identifier
        \\does not bind. The range types themselves are described in the chapter on
        \\ranges and slicing.
    );
    table_header(2, "Spelling   —   Interval matched   —   Prelude type");
    table_row(3, "`a..b`   |   `a` through `b - 1`   |   `Range(T)`");
    table_row(4, "`a..=b`   |   `a` through `b`   |   `RangeInclusive(T)`");
    table_row(5, "`a..`   |   `a` through the domain maximum   |   `RangeFrom(T)`");
    table_row(6, "`..b`   |   the domain minimum through `b - 1`   |   `RangeTo(T)`");
    table_row(7, "`..=b`   |   the domain minimum through `b`   |   `RangeToInclusive(T)`");
    table_row(8, "`..`   |   the whole domain   |   `RangeFull(T)`");
    paragraph(9, #string,oneline
        \\Every shape normalises to a closed interval, and the interval is then
        \\clamped to the subject's own domain. Both steps exist for the coverage
        \\analysis: it never has to reason about a half-open interval, and an open
        \\end needs no separate intrinsic for the type's extreme value, because the
        \\widest interval plus a clamp says exactly what `100..=max` would say.
        \\Clamping is also why a witness over a `u8` subject reads `10..=99` rather
        \\than a signed 64-bit span.
    );
    code_block(10, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\report :: (n: s64) {
        \\    print("n = %:", n);
        \\    if #pattern 0..3 = n   print(" 0..3");
        \\    if #pattern 0..=3 = n  print(" 0..=3");
        \\    if #pattern 5.. = n    print(" 5..");
        \\    if #pattern ..3 = n    print(" ..3");
        \\    if #pattern ..=3 = n   print(" ..=3");
        \\    if #pattern .. = n     print(" ..");
        \\    print("\n");
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    report(-1);
        \\    report(0);
        \\    report(3);
        \\    report(7);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(11, "text", #string
        \\n = -1: ..3 ..=3 ..
        \\n = 0: 0..3 0..=3 ..3 ..=3 ..
        \\n = 3: 0..=3 ..=3 ..
        \\n = 7: 5.. ..
    );
    paragraph(12, #string,oneline
        \\In a switch the same spellings are case labels. Because each one is a
        \\closed interval over an enumerable domain, a set of ranges that tiles the
        \\subject's type is proved total and needs no `else`.
    );
    code_block(13, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\grade :: (score: s64) -> string {
        \\    if #pattern score == {
        \\    case 90..;     return "A";
        \\    case 80..=89;  return "B";
        \\    case 70..=79;  return "C";
        \\    case ..=69;    return "F";
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("% % % %\n", grade(95), grade(83), grade(70), grade(12));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(14, "text", #string
        \\A B C F
    );
    subheading(15, "Subjects and bounds");
    paragraph(16, #string,oneline
        \\A bound must be known at compile time, because the interval is baked into
        \\the pattern the analysis reasons over. A runtime local as a bound is
        \\`error: a range bound must be known at compile time: unknown const hi`,
        \\naming the offending identifier. A non-integer bound is
        \\`error: a range bound must be an integer`, which is what a float range
        \\such as `0.0..=9.0` produces: the Prelude instances are all constrained
        \\`where Is_Integer(T)`.
    );
    paragraph(17, #string,oneline
        \\The subject must be an integer or an enum. Anything else is
        \\`error: a range pattern needs an integer subject, got string`. Enum
        \\subjects work because a discriminant is an integer underneath, and `#char`
        \\bounds work because a character literal is one too.
    );
    code_block(18, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Color :: enum { Red; Green; Blue; Violet; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    c := Color.Green;
        \\    if #pattern Color.Red..=Color.Blue = c  print("green is in the first three\n");
        \\    v := Color.Violet;
        \\    if #pattern Color.Red..=Color.Blue = v  print("unreached\n");
        \\    else print("violet is outside\n");
        \\
        \\    b: u8 = #char "q";
        \\    if #pattern #char "a" ..= #char "z" = b  print("lower-case byte\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(19, "text", #string
        \\green is in the first three
        \\violet is outside
        \\lower-case byte
    );
    subheading(20, "The `ToPattern` typeclass");
    paragraph(21, #string,oneline
        \\`ToPattern` is a one-method class declared in the Prelude. Its method
        \\takes its argument `#comptime`, since the whole point is to produce a
        \\pattern during checking, and returns the compile-time-only type
        \\`pattern`.
    );
    code_block(22, "glosso", #string
        \\ToPattern :: typeclass (T: type) {
        \\    to_pattern :: (#comptime value: T) -> pattern;
        \\}
        \\
        \\Range($T) :: instance ToPattern where Is_Integer(T) {
        \\    to_pattern :: (#comptime value: Range(T)) -> pattern #magic "range_to_pattern";
        \\}
        \\
        \\RangeInclusive($T) :: instance ToPattern where Is_Integer(T) {
        \\    to_pattern :: (#comptime value: RangeInclusive(T)) -> pattern #magic "range_inclusive_to_pattern";
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(23, #string,oneline
        \\`RangeFrom`, `RangeTo`, `RangeToInclusive` and `RangeFull` follow the
        \\same shape, each with its own intrinsic and each constrained
        \\`where Is_Integer(T)`. Those six instances are the specification of what a
        \\range means as a pattern. In practice the checker recognises the six
        \\built-in shapes from their syntax and builds the interval directly,
        \\because only the two bounds matter and they are plain integers; a user
        \\type is not special-cased away, and reaches its instance through an
        \\actual synthesized call to `to_pattern`.
    );
    subheading(24, "Writing an instance");
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\A user type joins the pattern language by declaring a `ToPattern`
        \\instance whose method returns a pattern quote. `#insert` in pattern
        \\position is the splice, both for a value that is already a `pattern` and
        \\for a value whose type has an instance, which is what makes
        \\`case #insert Ascii_Class.Digit;` legal against a `u8` subject.
    );
    code_block(26, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Ascii_Class :: enum { Digit; Alpha; }
        \\
        \\Ascii_Class :: instance ToPattern {
        \\    to_pattern :: (#comptime c: Ascii_Class) -> pattern {
        \\        if c == {
        \\        case .Digit; return to_pattern(#char "0" ..= #char "9");
        \\        case .Alpha; return #pattern #insert to_pattern(#char "a" ..= #char "z")
        \\                                    | #insert to_pattern(#char "A" ..= #char "Z");
        \\        }
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\describe :: (byte: u8) {
        \\    if #pattern byte == {
        \\    case #insert Ascii_Class.Digit;  print("digit\n");
        \\    case #insert Ascii_Class.Alpha;  print("letter\n");
        \\    else;                            print("other\n");
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    describe(#char "x");
        \\    describe(#char "7");
        \\    describe(#char "!");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(27, "text", #string
        \\letter
        \\digit
        \\other
    );
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\`#insert` is required. Written bare, `Ascii_Class.Digit` is an ordinary
        \\enum-constant pattern and is checked against the subject's type, giving
        \\`error: got Ascii_Class, expected u8`. Splicing a value whose type has no
        \\instance is `error: this case is not a pattern, and its type has no
        \\'ToPattern' instance`, followed by the candidate list for `to_pattern`.
    );
    paragraph(29, #string,oneline
        \\Because the conversion happens before the coverage analysis runs, an
        \\instance joins that analysis on the same footing as the built-in ranges.
        \\Dropping the `else` from `describe` above produces witnesses drawn from
        \\the intervals the instance returned, clamped to `u8`.
    );
    code_block(30, "text", #string
        \\error: non-exhaustive #pattern switch
        \\note: missing case: 0..=47
        \\note: missing case: 58..=64
        \\note: missing case: 91..=96
        \\note: missing case: 123..=255
        \\note: cover them, or add 'else' or '#partial'
    );
    note(31, #string,oneline
        \\A `pattern` value is compile-time only. `p := #pattern .Some(v);` is
        \\`error: a 'pattern' value is compile-time only; bind it with '::' rather
        \\than ':='`. The type and its introspection intrinsics belong to the
        \\chapter on patterns as compile-time values.
    );
}

render_section_94 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\Exhaustiveness in Glosso is computed, not demanded. The compiler runs
        \\a usefulness analysis over a matrix of deconstructed patterns: one row
        \\per arm, one column per position being matched. A row is useful if some
        \\value matches it that no earlier row already matches. The same routine
        \\answers both questions the language needs: a switch is exhaustive when
        \\the all-wildcard row is no longer useful against its arms, and arm `N` is
        \\reachable exactly when row `N` is useful against rows `0` through
        \\`N - 1`.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\When a switch is not exhaustive the analysis reconstructs a concrete
        \\value that no arm matches, by unspecializing on the way back out of the
        \\recursion, and prints it as a witness. A witness is a pattern, not a
        \\value, so it may contain `_` where any value would do.
    );
    paragraph(2, #string,oneline
        \\The analysis governs `#pattern` switches and the switch forms of `ifx`.
        \\`ifx` reuses the same witnesses under its own wording,
        \\`error: cannot decide a value for this ifx` followed by
        \\`note: missing case: Color.Blue` and
        \\`note: cover every case, or add an 'else' arm`; its chapter has the rest.
        \\A tagged-union statement switch is checked more crudely, by counting the
        \\tags an arm names, and reports
        \\`error: switch is exhaustive by default; add else or #partial`. A plain
        \\statement switch over an integer, string, float, boolean or enum subject
        \\is not coverage-checked at all; the chapter on switching on a value
        \\covers that.
    );
    subheading(3, "Enumerable and opaque domains");
    paragraph(4, #string,oneline
        \\Whether coverage can be proved depends on whether the subject's
        \\constructors can be enumerated. Three answers are possible: a finite set
        \\of constructors that can be listed, an infinite domain that only a
        \\wildcard covers, and a pattern the analysis cannot interpret as a
        \\constructor at all.
    );
    table_header(5, "Subject   —   Treated as   —   Covered by");
    table_row(6, "tagged union   |   one constructor per variant   |   every variant, or a wildcard");
    table_row(7, "enum   |   one constructor per discriminant   |   every constant, or a wildcard");
    table_row(8, "`bool`   |   two constructors   |   `true` and `false`, or a wildcard");
    table_row(9, "struct   |   one constructor, decided by its fields   |   its fields being covered");
    table_row(10, "integer   |   intervals, split rather than enumerated   |   intervals that tile the type");
    table_row(11, "float, string, pointer   |   infinite   |   only a wildcard");
    table_row(12, "literal, `==` constant, slice   |   opaque   |   nothing; they never cover");
    paragraph(13, #string,oneline
        \\The last row is the one that surprises. A float or string literal arm
        \\matches at run time but proves nothing at compile time, so a switch built
        \\entirely from them is never total and its witness is the bare wildcard
        \\`_`. The same holds for `==` constant patterns and for slice patterns:
        \\they are matched at run time, and absent from the coverage matrix.
    );
    subheading(14, "The diagnostic");
    code_block(15, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Shape :: union { Circle: f64; Rect: f64; Empty: void; }
        \\
        \\area :: (s: Shape) -> f64 {
        \\    if #pattern s == {
        \\    case Shape.Circle(r);  return 3.0 * r * r;
        \\    case Shape.Rect(w);    return w * w;
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () { print("%\n", area(Shape.Rect(2.0))); }
    );
    code_block(16, "text", #string
        \\error: non-exhaustive #pattern switch
        \\note: missing case: Shape.Empty
        \\note: cover them, or add 'else' or '#partial'
    );
    paragraph(17, #string,oneline
        \\The error is reported on the `if` and one `note: missing case:` line is
        \\emitted per witness, up to six, after which the count is summarised as
        \\`note: ...and N more`. The final note names the three ways out: cover the
        \\missing cases, add `else;`, or mark the switch `#partial`.
    );
    table_header(18, "Subject and arms   —   Witness");
    table_row(19, "`Shape` covered but for `Empty`   |   `Shape.Empty`");
    table_row(20, "`Shape` with `Circle(1.0)` and `Empty`   |   `Shape.Circle(_)`");
    table_row(21, "`Color { Red; Green; Blue }` with two arms   |   `Color.Blue`");
    table_row(22, "`bool` with `true`   |   `false`");
    table_row(23, "`s64` with `1` and `2`   |   `-9223372036854775808..=0`, `3..=9223372036854775807`");
    table_row(24, "`u8` with `0..=9` and `100..`   |   `10..=99`");
    table_row(25, "a two-`bool` struct with the two matching arms   |   `Pair.{ .first = false, .second = true }` and `Pair.{ .first = true, .second = false }`");
    table_row(26, "one `string` or `f64` literal   |   `_`");
    table_row(27, "subject-less predicate arms   |   `_`");
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\Two of those rows are worth reading twice. An integer subject yields
        \\intervals rather than a list of values, because the domain is split at
        \\the boundaries the arms mention instead of being enumerated. And a
        \\subject-less `#pattern` switch, whose arms are arbitrary boolean
        \\predicates, is never provably total: the witness is always `_`.
    );
    code_block(29, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Big :: enum { A; B; C; D; E; F; G; H; I; J; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    v := Big.A;
        \\    if #pattern v == { case Big.A; print("a\n"); }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(30, "text", #string
        \\error: non-exhaustive #pattern switch
        \\note: missing case: Big.B
        \\note: missing case: Big.C
        \\note: missing case: Big.D
        \\note: missing case: Big.E
        \\note: missing case: Big.F
        \\note: missing case: Big.G
        \\note: ...and 3 more
        \\note: cover them, or add 'else' or '#partial'
    );
    subheading(31, "How a guard affects coverage");
    paragraph(32, #string,oneline
        \\A guarded arm contributes nothing. The pattern may match and the
        \\guard may still decline at run time, so the arm proves no coverage and is
        \\absent from the matrix altogether. Covering a variant only under a guard
        \\therefore leaves it missing.
    );
    code_block(33, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Shape :: union { Circle: f64; Rect: f64; Empty: void; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    s: Shape = .Empty;
        \\    if #pattern s == {
        \\    case Shape.Circle(r) if r > 1.0;  print("big circle\n");
        \\    case Shape.Rect(w);               print("rect\n");
        \\    case Shape.Empty;                 print("empty\n");
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(34, "text", #string
        \\error: non-exhaustive #pattern switch
        \\note: missing case: Shape.Circle(_)
        \\note: cover them, or add 'else' or '#partial'
    );
    paragraph(35, #string,oneline
        \\`#falling` is the opposite case and is easy to confuse with a guard: a
        \\`#falling` arm does not decline its match, it adds the next arm's body to
        \\its own, so it covers its case exactly like an ordinary arm. The chapter
        \\on `case A, B;` groups and `#falling` has the rest.
    );
    subheading(36, "Reachability");
    paragraph(37, #string,oneline
        \\The same usefulness call, run against the arms that precede each arm,
        \\reports arms that can never be entered. This is a warning, not an
        \\error, and it runs whether or not the switch has an `else`. It applies
        \\only to `#pattern` switches with a subject; duplicate labels in a plain
        \\switch are not diagnosed, and the first matching arm wins.
    );
    code_block(38, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    n: u8 = 5;
        \\    if #pattern n == {
        \\    case 0..=99;   print("low\n");
        \\    case 50..=60;  print("mid\n");
        \\    else;          print("high\n");
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(39, "text", #string
        \\warning: unreachable pattern - already covered by an earlier case
        \\low
    );
    paragraph(40, #string,oneline
        \\A guarded arm is neither reported by this lint nor counted against later
        \\arms, for the same reason it contributes no coverage: it can decline. So
        \\`case any if false;` written first leaves every following arm reachable,
        \\while an unguarded `case any;` makes them all dead.
    );
    note(41, #string,oneline
        \\`#partial`, written immediately after `if` and before `#pattern`, waives
        \\the coverage requirement; control then falls out of the switch when
        \\nothing matches. It is the right answer when the uncovered cases are
        \\genuinely uninteresting, and the wrong answer when a later edit adds a
        \\variant, because `#partial` is exactly the annotation that stops the
        \\compiler from pointing at the new one.
    );
}

render_section_95 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\A case label may name several values at once: `case A, B;` runs its body
        \\when the subject matches any of the listed labels. The labels are values
        \\of the subject's type, so the form works for integers, strings, `#char`
        \\literals, floats, booleans, enum constants and union tags. It is one arm
        \\with several labels, not several arms.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Level :: enum { Trace; Debug; Info; Warn; Error; }
        \\
        \\classify :: (n: s64) -> string {
        \\    if n == {
        \\    case 2, 3, 5, 7;  return "small prime";
        \\    case 0, 1;        return "unit";
        \\    else;             return "other";
        \\    }
        \\    return "unreached";
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("% % %\n", classify(5), classify(1), classify(9));
        \\
        \\    lvl := Level.Warn;
        \\    if lvl == {
        \\    case .Trace, .Debug;  print("verbose\n");
        \\    case .Info;           print("normal\n");
        \\    case .Warn, .Error;   print("loud\n");
        \\    }
        \\
        \\    word := "yes";
        \\    if word == {
        \\    case "y", "yes", "true";  print("affirmative\n");
        \\    case "n", "no", "false";  print("negative\n");
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(2, "text", #string
        \\small prime unit other
        \\loud
        \\affirmative
    );
    subheading(3, "The no-binding restriction");
    paragraph(4, #string,oneline
        \\A multi-label case may not capture. The arm body would have to be checked
        \\against every listed tag at once, and the captured name would have a
        \\different type in each, so the compiler rejects the combination outright
        \\rather than picking one.
    );
    code_block(5, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Token :: union { Name: string; Text: string; Eof: void; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    t: Token = .Text("hi");
        \\    if t == {
        \\    case .Name(x), .Text(x);  print("stringy %\n", x);
        \\    case .Eof;                print("eof\n");
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(6, "text", #string
        \\error: a case with several tags cannot capture a payload - the tags have different payload types
    );
    paragraph(7, #string,oneline
        \\The diagnostic fires on the presence of a capture in a multi-tag case,
        \\not on a comparison of the payload types, so it appears even when the
        \\tags agree, as `Name` and `Text` do above. Grouping tags whose payloads
        \\are irrelevant is fine: `case .Eof, .Nil;` on two `void` variants
        \\compiles and covers both.
    );
    paragraph(8, #string,oneline
        \\The construct for one body over several shapes with a capture is the
        \\or-pattern in a `#pattern` switch, which requires the alternatives to
        \\bind the same names at the same types and so has no such problem.
    );
    code_block(9, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Shape :: union { Circle: f64; Rect: f64; Empty: void; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    s: Shape = .Rect(2.0);
        \\    if #pattern s == {
        \\    case Shape.Circle(r) | Shape.Rect(r);  print("sized %\n", r);
        \\    case Shape.Empty;                      print("empty\n");
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(10, "text", #string
        \\sized 2
    );
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\The comma spelling is not available in a `#pattern` switch:
        \\`case Shape.Circle(r), Shape.Rect(r);` there is
        \\`error: unexpected token ','`. A pattern case takes exactly one pattern,
        \\and `|` is how alternatives are written inside it.
    );
    subheading(12, "`#falling`");
    paragraph(13, #string,oneline
        \\`#falling;` gives C-style fall-through, with one meaning: it appends the
        \\next case's body to this one, unconditionally and without running
        \\that case's test. The arm does its own work and then the following arm's.
        \\It must be the last statement of the case body.
    );
    code_block(14, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    lvl := 1;
        \\    if lvl == {
        \\    case 1;  print("T"); #falling;
        \\    case 2;  print("D"); #falling;
        \\    case 3;  print("I");
        \\    else;    print("-");
        \\    }
        \\    print("\n");
        \\    lvl2 := 3;
        \\    if lvl2 == {
        \\    case 1;  print("T"); #falling;
        \\    case 2;  print("D"); #falling;
        \\    case 3;  print("I");
        \\    else;    print("-");
        \\    }
        \\    print("\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(15, "text", #string
        \\TDI
        \\I
    );
    paragraph(16, #string,oneline
        \\Four placements are rejected, each with its own message. Anything written
        \\after `#falling` in the same body is unreachable by construction, so the
        \\parser refuses it; an `else` arm and the final case have no following body
        \\to enter. The fourth is the restriction on binding switches, developed
        \\below.
    );
    table_header(17, "Placement   —   Diagnostic");
    table_row(18, "not the last statement of the case   |   `error: '#falling' must be the last statement of a case`");
    table_row(19, "on the `else` arm   |   `error: '#falling' on an 'else' arm has nothing to fall into`");
    table_row(20, "on the last case   |   `error: '#falling' on the last case has no body to fall into`");
    table_row(21, "in a switch whose cases bind   |   `error: '#falling' enters the next case's body without running its test, so it is not allowed in a switch whose cases can bind`");
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\The last-case error carries `note: it would leave the switch on every
        \\subject that reaches it; delete it`. A `#falling` with nowhere to fall is
        \\not a subtle mistake: it turns the arm into a no-op exit on every subject
        \\that reaches it, and the compiler can see that at check time.
    );
    subheading(23, "Where `#falling` is allowed");
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\Entering a body whose test never ran is harmless when that body binds
        \\nothing, and unsound the moment it binds something: the body would read a
        \\payload from a variant it was not dispatched on. The rule follows the
        \\hazard rather than trying to re-test. Two switch forms can bind and are
        \\excluded outright: the tagged-union switch and every `#pattern` switch.
        \\What remains is the plain value switch over integers, strings, characters,
        \\floats and booleans, and the enum switch, which is what the feature is
        \\for.
    );
    code_block(25, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Token :: union { Name: string; Code: s64; Eof: void; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    t: Token = .Code(3);
        \\    if t == {
        \\    case .Name(s);  print("name %\n", s); #falling;
        \\    case .Code(v);  print("code %\n", v);
        \\    case .Eof;      print("eof\n");
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(26, "text", #string
        \\error: '#falling' enters the next case's body without running its test, so it is not allowed in a switch whose cases can bind
        \\note: S8 requires a tagged union's payload to be reached through a capture in the arm that proved the tag, so every case here binds
        \\note: group the tags with 'case A, B;' if they share a body, or lift the shared work into a function
    );
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\In a `#pattern` switch the first note reads
        \\`note: a #pattern switch exists to destructure, so its cases bind`
        \\instead. The second note is the same in both, and is the whole remedy:
        \\group the labels, or move the shared work into a procedure both arms
        \\call.
    );
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\A `#falling` arm still covers its own case. It always runs its own
        \\body, so it is an ordinary row in the coverage matrix, unlike a guarded
        \\arm; the chapter on exhaustiveness, guards, and reachability draws that
        \\distinction. `#falling` also composes with a grouped label.
    );
    code_block(29, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Level :: enum { Trace; Debug; Info; Warn; Error; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    lvl := Level.Info;
        \\    if lvl == {
        \\    case .Trace, .Debug;  print("verbose"); #falling;
        \\    case .Info;           print(" info\n");
        \\    case .Warn, .Error;   print("loud\n");
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(30, "text", #string
        \\ info
    );
    note(31, #string,oneline
        \\`#falling` means something different in a `#if` switch: a static
        \\`#falling` arm re-tests from the next case rather than entering its body.
        \\The chapter on static branching with `#if` covers that form.
    );
}

render_section_96 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\`#pattern P := subject else { ... }` binds `P`'s captures into the
        \\enclosing scope and requires the `else` block to leave. It exists
        \\because the ordinary test nests: every optional unwrapped with
        \\`if #pattern .Some(v) = e { ... }` pushes the rest of the procedure one
        \\block deeper, and a procedure that unwraps four things ends up written
        \\four levels in, with the failure handling scattered down the right
        \\margin.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\The bind form inverts that. The failure path is stated once, immediately,
        \\and the success path continues at the same indentation with the captures
        \\live from that statement to the end of the enclosing block.
    );
    code_block(2, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Config :: struct { name: string; port: s64; }
        \\
        \\lookup :: (found: bool) -> ?Config {
        \\    if found return Config.{ .name = "svc", .port = 8080 };
        \\    return .None;
        \\}
        \\
        \\serve :: (found: bool) -> s64 {
        \\    #pattern .Some(cfg) := lookup(found) else { return -1; }
        \\    print("serving % on %\n", cfg.name, cfg.port);
        \\    return cfg.port;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("ok = %\n", serve(true));
        \\    print("missing = %\n", serve(false));
        \\
        \\    xs: [3]?s64 = .[.Some(1), .None, .Some(3)];
        \\    total := 0;
        \\    for x: xs {
        \\        #pattern .Some(v) := x else { continue; }
        \\        total += v;
        \\    }
        \\    print("total = %\n", total);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(3, "text", #string
        \\serving svc on 8080
        \\ok = 8080
        \\missing = -1
        \\total = 4
    );
    paragraph(4, #string,oneline
        \\`cfg` is an ordinary local from the statement onwards, not a name that
        \\lives only inside a nested block. Inside a loop the `else` may
        \\`continue`, which turns the form into "skip the elements that are not
        \\there", as the `total = 4` line shows: the `.None` element is skipped and
        \\`1 + 3` remains.
    );
    subheading(5, "Why the `else` must diverge");
    paragraph(6, #string,oneline
        \\Binding into the enclosing scope is only sound if the code after the
        \\statement is unreachable when the match failed. That is exactly what
        \\requiring the `else` to leave buys: there is no path on which `cfg` is
        \\read without having been written. The requirement is not a style rule, it
        \\is the premise the scoping decision rests on.
    );
    code_block(7, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    o: ?s64 = .Some(3);
        \\    #pattern .Some(v) := o else { print("absent\n"); }
        \\    print("v = %\n", v);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(8, "text", #string
        \\error: the 'else' of a '#pattern ... :=' binding must not return normally
        \\note: it has to be #noreturn - end it with 'return', 'break', 'continue', or a call to a #noreturn function
    );
    paragraph(9, #string,oneline
        \\For the same reason the `else` block is checked without the bindings.
        \\They do not exist on the failure path, so naming one there is
        \\`error: unbound 'v'`, with the usual `help: did you mean 'o'?` suggestion
        \\pointing at the subject.
    );
    subheading(10, "What counts as diverging");
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\The check is a syntactic walk over the block's statements, asking whether
        \\control can reach the end. Any of the following forms as the last thing
        \\the block does satisfies it.
    );
    table_header(12, "Form   —   Diverges   —   Why");
    table_row(13, "`return`, `return e`   |   yes   |   leaves the procedure");
    table_row(14, "`break`, `continue`   |   yes   |   leaves the enclosing loop iteration");
    table_row(15, "`goto('label)`   |   yes   |   `goto` is declared `#noreturn`");
    table_row(16, "a call to a `#noreturn` procedure   |   yes   |   the declaration says it does not return");
    table_row(17, "`if` with both branches diverging   |   yes   |   neither path reaches the end");
    table_row(18, "an exhaustive switch, all arms diverging   |   yes   |   every arm leaves and one always runs");
    table_row(19, "`while true { }`   |   no   |   the walk does not evaluate the condition");
    paragraph(20, #string,oneline
        \\The last row is the trap. An infinite loop plainly never returns, but the
        \\check does not reason about loop conditions, so
        \\`#pattern .Some(v) := o else { while true { } }` is rejected with the
        \\same "must not return normally" error. Write an explicit `return` or a
        \\call to a `#noreturn` procedure instead.
    );
    code_block(21, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Jump";
        \\
        \\bail :: (msg: string) #noreturn { print("fatal: %\n", msg); exit(2); }
        \\
        \\both_ways :: (o: ?s64, flag: bool) -> s64 {
        \\    #pattern .Some(v) := o else { if flag { return -1; } else { return -2; } }
        \\    return v;
        \\}
        \\
        \\by_bail :: (o: ?s64) -> s64 {
        \\    #pattern .Some(v) := o else { bail("no value"); }
        \\    return v;
        \\}
        \\
        \\by_goto :: (o: ?s64) -> s64 {
        \\    #pattern .Some(v) := o else { goto('fail); }
        \\    return v;
        \\'fail;
        \\    return -3;
        \\}
        \\
        \\by_switch :: (o: ?s64, k: s64) -> s64 {
        \\    #pattern .Some(v) := o else {
        \\        if k == {
        \\        case 1;  return -1;
        \\        else;    return -9;
        \\        }
        \\    }
        \\    return v;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("% % % %\n", both_ways(.None, true), by_goto(.None),
        \\                       by_switch(.None, 1), by_switch(.None, 7));
        \\    print("%\n", by_bail(.Some(5)));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(22, "text", #string
        \\-1 -3 -1 -9
        \\5
    );
    subheading(23, "Shape and restrictions");
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\The `else` is mandatory and must be a block. Omitting it is
        \\`error: unexpected token ';'` at the end of the statement, and writing a
        \\single statement instead of a block, as in `else return -1;`, is
        \\`error: unexpected token 'return'`. A trailing `;` after the block is
        \\optional, as everywhere else.
    );
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\The parser decides between the bind form, the test form and the quote by
        \\scanning ahead for a `:=` at nesting depth zero. That scan counts `(`,
        \\`[` and `{` as openers but does not count `.{` or `.[`, so a top-level
        \\struct or slice pattern makes the scan stop early and the statement is
        \\read as a test. Both of the following are `error: unexpected token ':='`.
    );
    code_block(26, "glosso", #string
        \\#pattern Pair.{ .first = a, .second = b } := p else { return -1; }
        \\#pattern .[a, b] := xs else { return -1; }
    );
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\A variant pattern is unaffected, because its payload sits inside `(` and
        \\`)`, so the practical form is to bind the payload and destructure it
        \\afterwards: `#pattern .Some(p) := o else { return -1; }` accepts a
        \\`?Pair` and leaves `p` a plain `Pair`. `whole @ .Some(v) := o` also
        \\parses, for the same reason.
    );
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\Every pattern shape is otherwise available, and the subject may be any
        \\expression. `?T` and `E!T` are the common subjects: `#pattern .Ok(v) :=
        \\parse(text) else { return -1; }` is the error-handling spelling for a
        \\procedure that does not want to propagate, which the chapter on `Try`,
        \\`?`, and `#try` contrasts with propagation.
    );
    note(29, #string,oneline
        \\The bind form is the answer the compiler points at when a pattern test is
        \\negated. `if !(#pattern .Some(v) = o)` is rejected with
        \\`note: to bind and leave on failure, write '#pattern P := subject else {
        \\... }'`, because inverting a test would leave its captures with no branch
        \\to bind into.
    );
}

render_section_97 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\A `#pattern P` written without `= subject` is a quote rather than a
        \\test: it produces a value of the built-in type `pattern` that carries
        \\the pattern's syntax. The parser decides between the two by scanning
        \\ahead for a depth-zero `=`. `==` is a different token, so a constant
        \\pattern such as `#pattern == LIMIT` is never mistaken for a test.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\A pattern was the one piece of Glosso syntax that could not be named. A
        \\predicate can be a procedure, a type can be a value, a block can be a
        \\`Code` value, but a pattern had to be written out in full at every site
        \\that matched it. The quote names it; `#insert` in pattern position puts
        \\it back.
    );
    code_block(2, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Token :: union { Number: f64; Name: string; Eof: void; }
        \\
        \\NUMBER :: #pattern Token.Number(n);
        \\NAME   :: #pattern Token.Name(text);
        \\
        \\describe :: (t: Token) {
        \\    if #pattern t == {
        \\    case #insert NUMBER;   print("number %\n", n);
        \\    case #insert NAME;     print("name %\n", text);
        \\    case Token.Eof;        print("eof\n");
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    describe(Token.Number(2.5));
        \\    describe(Token.Name("hi"));
        \\    describe(Token.Eof());
        \\}
    );
    code_block(3, "text", #string
        \\number 2.5
        \\name hi
        \\eof
    );
    paragraph(4, #string,oneline
        \\A quote carries its binders with it. The arm that spliced `NUMBER`
        \\reads `n` and the arm that spliced `NAME` reads `text`, under the names
        \\the quote wrote, in the scope the splice landed in. Everything the
        \\pattern vocabulary accepts may be quoted; the vocabulary itself is in
        \\The Pattern Vocabulary.
    );

    subheading(5, "A pattern has no storage");
    paragraph(6, #string,oneline
        \\`pattern` is a compile-time-only type. `size_of(pattern)` is `0`, its
        \\values are dropped from every runtime argument list, and reaching
        \\lowering with one is an error. The checker rejects them where they are
        \\written rather than where they are first used, which is why the
        \\diagnostics below point at the declaration.
    );
    paragraph(7, #string,oneline
        \\A pattern must therefore be bound with `::`, the compile-time constant
        \\form, and may not be stored in an aggregate or in a runtime variable.
    );
    bullet(8, #string,oneline
        \\`p := #pattern .Some(v);` is `error: a 'pattern' value is compile-time
        \\only; bind it with '::' rather than ':='`.
    );
    bullet(9, #string,oneline
        \\`Holder :: struct { p: pattern; }` is `error: field 'p' has the
        \\compile-time-only type 'pattern', which has no storage`.
    );
    bullet(10, #string,oneline
        \\A quote written where a test was meant, as in `if #pattern n { ... }`,
        \\reaches the lowerer and is `error: cannot lower PatternQuote
        \\expression`.
    );
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\`Code` is the other compile-time-only type and obeys the same rules;
        \\the two share their machinery. `pattern` differs in two ways, both
        \\covered below: identity is structural rather than by node, and a splice
        \\deep-clones instead of adopting the quoted nodes.
    );

    subheading(12, "Splicing with `#insert`");
    paragraph(13, #string,oneline
        \\`#insert e` where a pattern is expected splices the pattern that `e`
        \\evaluates to. `#insert` keeps its single meaning, "splice this
        \\compile-time syntax value here", and the position selects which kind of
        \\syntax is expected. Every pattern position accepts it: the test form,
        \\a case label, the bind-or-diverge form, and the inside of another
        \\quote.
    );
    paragraph(14, #string,oneline
        \\A splice binds tighter than `|` and at the same level as the `==`
        \\constant marker, and looser than the postfix forms. `#insert A |
        \\#insert B` is therefore an or-pattern over two splices rather than one
        \\bitwise-or expression.
    );
    code_block(15, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Shape :: union { Circle: f64; Rect: f64; Empty: void; }
        \\
        \\CIRCLE :: #pattern Shape.Circle(r);
        \\RECT   :: #pattern Shape.Rect(r);
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    s: Shape = .Rect(3.0);
        \\    if #pattern #insert CIRCLE | #insert RECT = s print("either r=%\n", r);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(16, "text", "either r=3");
    paragraph(17, #string,oneline
        \\The splice deep-clones the quoted syntax at every site. A `Code` value
        \\spliced at two differently typed sites reuses the same nodes, and the
        \\types stamped by the first check survive into the second; a pattern is
        \\matched against a type by definition, so it cannot afford that. A
        \\spliced value that is not already a `pattern` is converted through its
        \\type's `ToPattern` instance, which is how a range enters a pattern; see
        \\Ranges in Patterns and `ToPattern`.
    );

    subheading(18, "Patterns as arguments");
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\A procedure takes a pattern through a `#comptime` parameter. The
        \\argument is const-evaluated at the call site and bound into the
        \\callee's compile-time environment, which is what lets the callee splice
        \\it.
    );
    code_block(20, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Token :: union { Number: f64; Name: string; Eof: void; }
        \\
        \\EOF_PATTERN :: #pattern Token.Eof;
        \\
        \\matches :: (#comptime p: pattern, t: Token) -> bool { return #pattern #insert p = t; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    t := Token.Eof();
        \\    print("% % %\n", matches(EOF_PATTERN, t), matches(#pattern Token.Eof, t),
        \\                     matches(#pattern Token.Name(s), t));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(21, "text", "1 1 0");
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\Pattern identity is structural. The monomorphization key is a rendering
        \\of node kinds and parser-set attributes only, never node ids and never
        \\spans, so two quotes that read the same are one instantiation. The
        \\three calls above are two: `--backend llvm-ir` emits two definitions of
        \\`matches`, because `EOF_PATTERN` and the quote written at the call site
        \\render identically.
    );
    code_block(23, "text", #string
        \\define i1 @glo_matches__pattern_Token__comptime_8a4b84710d3e5507
        \\define i1 @glo_matches__pattern_Token__comptime_fc76ffc729883eba
    );

    subheading(24, "The two binding tiers");
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\A bare identifier in a pattern binds, so a quote needs a way to say
        \\"this name is a name". Two markers do it, the pair `Code` already has.
        \\A single-backtick name is a name rather than a binder: it is read as an
        \\ordinary constant when the pattern is checked. A double-backtick name
        \\is evaluated in the environment the quote was written in, and the value
        \\it had is baked into the quote.
    );
    table_header(26, "Spelling   —   In a pattern   —   Reads from");
    table_row(27, "bare name   |   binds a fresh capture   |   nothing; it is a binder");
    table_row(28, "one backtick   |   a name, not a binder   |   the file's constants");
    table_row(29, "two backticks   |   the value that name held   |   the quoting environment");
    code_block(30, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\MAX :: 255;
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    n := 255;
        \\    m := 7;
        \\    print("backtick name vs MAX: % %\n", #pattern `MAX = n, #pattern `MAX = m);
        \\    print("bare ident binds:     % %\n", #pattern MAX = n, #pattern MAX = m);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(31, "text", #string
        \\backtick name vs MAX: 1 0
        \\bare ident binds:     1 1
    );
    paragraph(32, #string,oneline
        \\The eager tier is what lets a quote built inside a generic carry a
        \\value that does not exist at the splice site. `exactly` below is
        \\evaluated at compile time with `v` bound to `2.5`; the pattern it
        \\returns holds `2.5`, not a reference to a parameter named `v`.
    );
    code_block(33, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Token :: union { Number: f64; Name: string; Eof: void; }
        \\
        \\exactly :: (#comptime v: f64) -> pattern {
        \\    return #pattern Token.Number(== ``v);
        \\}
        \\
        \\EXACT :: #comptime exactly(2.5);
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("2.5 -> %\n", #pattern #insert EXACT = Token.Number(2.5));
        \\    print("1.0 -> %\n", #pattern #insert EXACT = Token.Number(1.0));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(34, "text", #string
        \\2.5 -> 1
        \\1.0 -> 0
    );
    note(35, #string,oneline
        \\A single-backtick name is looked up among the file's compile-time
        \\constants, not among the locals of the procedure that splices the
        \\pattern. A quote that names a constant declared inside that procedure
        \\is `error: unbound 'LIMIT'`, reported at the quote. Where the value is
        \\known at quote time, the double-backtick tier avoids the question
        \\entirely.
    );

    subheading(36, "The pattern intrinsics");
    paragraph(37, #string,oneline
        \\Six intrinsics in `std/Meta/Utilities.glo` read a pattern. Each is a
        \\compile-time `#magic` declaration, shown here without its directives.
        \\The subject type comes first in every one of them because a binder's
        \\type depends on both the pattern and the type it is matched against:
        \\`Token.Number(n)` binds `n` as `f64` only against `Token`.
    );
    code_block(38, "glosso", #string
        \\pattern_binder_count  :: (T: type, #comptime P: pattern) -> s64
        \\pattern_binder_name   :: (T: type, #comptime P: pattern, i: s64) -> string
        \\pattern_binder_type   :: (T: type, #comptime P: pattern, i: s64) -> type
        \\pattern_missing_count :: (T: type, Ps: []pattern) -> s64
        \\pattern_missing       :: (T: type, Ps: []pattern, i: s64) -> string
        \\pattern_is_useful     :: (T: type, Ps: []pattern, #comptime Q: pattern) -> bool
    );
    code_block(39, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Meta";
        \\
        \\Token :: union { Number: f64; Name: string; Eof: void; }
        \\
        \\NUMBER :: #pattern Token.Number(n);
        \\NAME   :: #pattern Token.Name(text);
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("binder count = %\n", pattern_binder_count(Token, NUMBER));
        \\    print("binder 0 = % : %\n", pattern_binder_name(Token, NUMBER, 0),
        \\                                type_name(pattern_binder_type(Token, NUMBER, 0)));
        \\    print("missing count = %\n", pattern_missing_count(Token, .[NUMBER, NAME]));
        \\    print("missing 0 = %\n", pattern_missing(Token, .[NUMBER, NAME], 0));
        \\    print("useful = %\n",  pattern_is_useful(Token, .[NUMBER], #pattern Token.Number(2.0)));
        \\    print("useful2 = %\n", pattern_is_useful(Token, .[NUMBER], #pattern Token.Eof));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(40, "text", #string
        \\binder count = 1
        \\binder 0 = n : f64
        \\missing count = 1
        \\missing 0 = Token.Eof
        \\useful = 0
        \\useful2 = 1
    );
    paragraph(41, #string,oneline
        \\`pattern_missing_count` and `pattern_is_useful` are the coverage
        \\analysis, asked directly. The contract is one-sided: the analysis errs
        \\only towards reporting more missing cases than a reader would, so a
        \\count of zero is a genuine totality proof and a non-zero count means
        \\"not proved" rather than "not total". Scalars, guarded arms and raw
        \\unions are the usual reasons for the difference; Exhaustiveness,
        \\Guards, and Reachability describes them, and Pattern Reflection covers
        \\the wider reflection surface.
    );
}

render_section_98 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\`#if` chooses between branches while the program is being checked. The
        \\condition is const-evaluated, the selected branch is spliced in, and
        \\the branches that were not selected are neither type-checked nor
        \\lowered. That last property is the whole point: a branch written for
        \\another target may name procedures that do not exist on this one.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\There are two spellings, matching the two runtime forms. The
        \\conditional form takes a `bool` condition and an optional `else`; the
        \\switch form takes a subject and `==` followed immediately by a brace,
        \\with `case` labels and an optional `else;` arm. Neither takes
        \\parentheses, and both accept a single statement in place of a block.
    );
    code_block(2, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    #if OUT.os == Target_OS.Windows print("single statement, no braces\n");
        \\
        \\    #if OUT.os == Target_OS.Windows {
        \\        print("windows\n");
        \\    } else #if OUT.os == Target_OS.Linux {
        \\        print("linux\n");
        \\    } else {
        \\        print("something else\n");
        \\    }
        \\
        \\    #if OUT.cpu == {
        \\    case .X86_64;             print("64-bit x86\n");
        \\    case .Aarch64, .Armv7;    print("arm\n");
        \\    else;                     print("other cpu\n");
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(3, "text", #string
        \\single statement, no braces
        \\windows
        \\64-bit x86
    );
    note(4, #string,oneline
        \\A static chain continues with `else #if`. Written `else if`, without
        \\the directive, the else branch is one ordinary runtime `if` statement:
        \\if the chain selects it, its condition is evaluated at run time and may
        \\read runtime locals, and both of its arms are checked and lowered.
    );

    subheading(5, "The condition");
    paragraph(6, #string,oneline
        \\The condition must be `bool`. `#if` does not take the truthiness rule
        \\that `if` takes, so `#if 1 { ... }` is `error: #if condition must be
        \\bool, got comptime_int`. The reason is that a static branch is a
        \\decision about the program text, and a silent coercion there would
        \\delete code on the strength of a guess.
    );
    paragraph(7, #string,oneline
        \\The condition must also be compile-time evaluable. A runtime local
        \\reports `error: unknown const i` against the name it could not read.
        \\What qualifies is anything the constant evaluator can reach: `::`
        \\constants, `#comptime` parameters, compile-time intrinsics such as
        \\`Is_Integer(T)` and `size_of(T)`, and the target constants below.
    );
    bullet(8, #string,oneline
        \\A file-scope `::=` binding is not entered into the compile-time
        \\environment, only local ones are, so `G ::= 7` at top level followed by
        \\`#if G == 7` is `error: unknown const G`. Use `::` for a file-scope
        \\constant.
    );

    subheading(9, "Only the selected branch is checked");
    paragraph(10, #string,oneline
        \\The branch that was not selected is stored and then ignored. It is
        \\parsed, so it must be syntactically well-formed, but no name in it is
        \\resolved and no type in it is computed.
    );
    code_block(11, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\WIDE :: true;
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    #if WIDE {
        \\        print("selected\n");
        \\    } else {
        \\        this_name_does_not_exist(1, 2, 3);
        \\    }
        \\
        \\    #if OUT.os == {
        \\    case .Windows;  print("windows\n");
        \\    else;           nonsense := "x" + 1;
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(12, "text", #string
        \\selected
        \\windows
    );
    paragraph(13, #string,oneline
        \\The same property makes `#if` the instantiation guard for generic code.
        \\A `#compile_error` in the branch that a bad instantiation selects turns
        \\a deep type error into one sentence at the call site.
    );
    code_block(14, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Meta";
        \\
        \\only_ints :: (x: $T) -> T {
        \\    #if !Is_Integer(T) { #compile_error "only_ints requires an integer"; }
        \\    return x + x;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () { print("%\n", only_ints(21)); }
    );
    code_block(15, "text", "42");
    paragraph(16, #string,oneline
        \\`only_ints(1.5)` selects the other branch and reports `error: only_ints
        \\requires an integer` at the `#compile_error`. Constraints that belong
        \\to the signature rather than the body are better written with `where`;
        \\see Constraints with `where`.
    );

    subheading(17, "`OUT` and `HOST`");
    paragraph(18, #string,oneline
        \\Two constants are installed by the compiler and are the usual subjects
        \\of a `#if`. They are ordinary struct values of the types declared in
        \\`std/Prelude/Utilities.glo`, so their fields are read with `.`.
    );
    table_header(19, "Constant   —   Fields   —   Describes");
    table_row(20, "`OUT`   |   `.os`, `.cpu`, `.abi`   |   the target being built");
    table_row(21, "`HOST`   |   `.os`, `.cpu`   |   the machine running the compiler");
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\The field types are the enums `Target_OS`, `Target_CPU` and
        \\`Target_ABI`; their members and the flags that set them are in Targets
        \\and Cross-Compilation. The standard library uses exactly this shape,
        \\for instance to answer the byte order of the target:
    );
    code_block(23, "glosso", #string
        \\target_endian :: () -> Target_Endian {
        \\    #if OUT.cpu == {
        \\    case .Powerpc, .Powerpc64, .S390x, .Mips, .Mips64;    return .Big;
        \\    else;            return .Little;
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\`OUT` and `HOST` differ whenever the build is a cross-compilation. A
        \\`#comptime` block runs on the host and a compiled procedure runs on the
        \\target, so a gate around a system call in ordinary code reads `OUT`,
        \\and a gate around something the compiler itself will do reads `HOST`.
    );

    subheading(25, "The limits");
    paragraph(26, #string,oneline
        \\`#if` is a statement. At file scope it is not a declaration and the
        \\parser says so: `error: bad decl name 'if'`. Nothing in `std/` uses a
        \\top-level `#if`; target gates are written inside a procedure body.
    );
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\`#if` cannot conditionally declare. A nested procedure, struct, enum,
        \\union or `distinct` declaration is hoisted to file scope by the parser,
        \\before any condition is evaluated, so it escapes the branch it was
        \\written in and exists whether or not that branch was selected.
    );
    code_block(28, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    #if false {
        \\        dead :: () { print("declared inside a dead branch\n"); }
        \\    }
        \\    dead();
        \\}
    );
    code_block(29, "text", "declared inside a dead branch");
    note(30, #string,oneline
        \\`#if false` removes statements, not declarations. To compile a
        \\procedure out of a build, guard its body, or select between two whole
        \\files with the load and import machinery described in Source Files,
        \\Imports, and Loads.
    );
    paragraph(31, #string,oneline
        \\A braced `#if` branch does not open a scope of its own. The selected
        \\block's statements are spliced into the enclosing block, so a variable
        \\declared in the taken branch is visible after it. This is a
        \\consequence of splicing rather than a decision, and it is what lets a
        \\`#if` chain define one name three different ways.
    );
    code_block(32, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    #if true { v := 5; }
        \\    print("%\n", v);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(33, "text", "5");
    paragraph(34, #string,oneline
        \\`#falling` means something different here. In a runtime switch it
        \\enters the next case's body unconditionally; in a `#if` switch it
        \\re-tests from the next case, so an arm that falls out of a static
        \\switch whose remaining labels do not match lands in `else`.
    );
    code_block(35, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    #if OUT.cpu == {
        \\    case .X86_64;   print("x86_64\n"); #falling;
        \\    case .Aarch64;  print("aarch64\n");
        \\    else;           print("re-tested into else\n");
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(36, "text", #string
        \\x86_64
        \\re-tested into else
    );
    table_header(37, "Question   —   `if`   —   `#if`");
    table_row(38, "condition   |   any truthy value   |   must be `bool`");
    table_row(39, "evaluated   |   at run time   |   at compile time");
    table_row(40, "other branch   |   checked, lowered   |   neither");
    table_row(41, "runtime locals   |   yes   |   no");
    table_row(42, "`#falling`   |   enters the next body   |   re-tests the next case");
    table_row(43, "file scope   |   n/a   |   not allowed");
}

render_section_99 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\`while` takes a condition and a body, and re-tests the condition before
        \\every iteration. The body may be a block or a single statement, and the
        \\condition is not parenthesised; parentheses around it are ordinary
        \\grouping.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\The condition is mandatory. `while { ... }` is `error: unexpected token
        \\'{'`, because the parser reads an expression immediately after the
        \\keyword and a block is not one. The infinite loop is spelled
        \\`while true`.
    );
    code_block(2, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    i := 0;
        \\    while i < 3 { print("i=%\n", i); i += 1; }
        \\
        \\    j := 0;
        \\    while j < 2 j += 1;
        \\    print("j=%\n", j);
        \\
        \\    k := 0;
        \\    while true { k += 1; if k == 4 break; }
        \\    print("k=%\n", k);
        \\
        \\    seen := 0;
        \\    n := 0;
        \\    while n < 5 { n += 1; if n % 2 == 0 continue; seen += n; }
        \\    print("seen=%\n", seen);
        \\
        \\    countdown := 3;
        \\    while countdown { countdown -= 1; }
        \\    print("countdown=%\n", countdown);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(3, "text", #string
        \\i=0
        \\i=1
        \\i=2
        \\j=2
        \\k=4
        \\seen=9
        \\countdown=0
    );
    paragraph(4, #string,oneline
        \\The condition follows the same truthiness rule as `if`, so the integer
        \\`countdown` above is a legal condition and stops the loop at zero. The
        \\full set of falsy values is in `if`, `else`, and Truthiness.
    );
    bullet(5, #string,oneline
        \\`break` leaves the innermost loop and `continue` starts its next
        \\iteration. Neither takes an argument and neither takes a label; to
        \\leave a nested loop, jump with `goto`, described in Labels and `goto`.
    );
    bullet(6, #string,oneline
        \\A `defer` written in the body runs at the end of every iteration,
        \\including the iteration that `break`s or `continue`s out of it.
    );
    bullet(7, #string,oneline
        \\A loop that never terminates is not diagnosed, and neither is a
        \\procedure that ends in one without returning; a function that runs off
        \\its end returns the zero value.
    );

    subheading(8, "Pattern conditions");
    paragraph(9, #string,oneline
        \\The condition may be a `#pattern` test. The captures reach the loop
        \\body and are re-bound from scratch on every iteration, which makes the
        \\form the natural spelling for walking an optional-terminated structure.
    );
    code_block(10, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Node :: struct { value: s64; next: ?*Node; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    opt: ?s64 = .Some(3);
        \\    while #pattern .Some(v) = opt {
        \\        print("v=%\n", v);
        \\        if v <= 1 opt = .None; else opt = .Some(v - 1);
        \\    }
        \\
        \\    o: ?s64 = .Some(4);
        \\    steps := 0;
        \\    while #pattern .Some(v) = o && v > 2 { steps += 1; o = .Some(v - 1); }
        \\    print("steps=%\n", steps);
        \\
        \\    c: Node = .{ .value = 3, .next = .None };
        \\    b: Node = .{ .value = 2, .next = .Some(*c) };
        \\    a: Node = .{ .value = 1, .next = .Some(*b) };
        \\    total := 0;
        \\    cursor: ?*Node = .Some(*a);
        \\    while #pattern .Some(p) = cursor { total += p.*.value; cursor = p.*.next; }
        \\    print("total=%\n", total);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(11, "text", #string
        \\v=3
        \\v=2
        \\v=1
        \\steps=2
        \\total=6
    );
    paragraph(12, #string,oneline
        \\The subject side of a test is parsed at a precedence above `&&` and
        \\`||`, so `while #pattern .Some(v) = o && v > 2` reads as
        \\`(#pattern .Some(v) = o) && v > 2`: the test runs first and the guard
        \\sees its capture.
    );
    paragraph(13, #string,oneline
        \\The multi-arm block form takes several tests against several subjects.
        \\Every arm must match for the loop to run, and every arm is re-tested on
        \\every iteration, so the loop stops as soon as any one of them fails.
    );
    code_block(14, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    left:  ?s64 = .Some(3);
        \\    right: ?s64 = .Some(10);
        \\    while #pattern {
        \\        .Some(l) = left;
        \\        .Some(r) = right;
        \\    } {
        \\        print("l=% r=%\n", l, r);
        \\        if l == 1 left = .None; else left = .Some(l - 1);
        \\        right = .Some(r + 10);
        \\    }
        \\    print("stopped\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(15, "text", #string
        \\l=3 r=10
        \\l=2 r=20
        \\l=1 r=30
        \\stopped
    );
    note(16, #string,oneline
        \\Two pattern tests must not be joined with `||`. The combination
        \\compiles, but `||` short-circuits, so the alternative that did not run
        \\leaves its captures in scope and never writes them, and a body that
        \\reads them sees zeroes. `&&` between tests is sound, and so is the
        \\multi-arm block above.
    );

    subheading(17, "`#inline while`");
    paragraph(18, #string,oneline
        \\`#inline while` is a compile-time loop. The condition is re-evaluated
        \\in the compile-time environment and the body is unrolled into
        \\straight-line statements until the condition is false. Nothing about
        \\the loop survives into the program.
    );
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\The counter must therefore be a compile-time binding. `i ::= 0`
        \\declares one; a plain `i := 0` is an ordinary runtime variable and the
        \\condition that reads it is `error: #inline while condition must be
        \\compile-time evaluable`.
    );
    code_block(20, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Meta";
        \\
        \\Vec3 :: struct { x: f32; y: f32; z: f32; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    sum := 0;
        \\    i ::= 0;
        \\    #inline while i < 4 { sum += i; i += 1; }
        \\    print("sum=%\n", sum);
        \\
        \\    k ::= 0;
        \\    #inline while k < field_count(Vec3) {
        \\        print("field %\n", field_name(Vec3, k));
        \\        k += 1;
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(21, "text", #string
        \\sum=6
        \\field x
        \\field y
        \\field z
    );
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\The second loop is what `#inline while` is for. `field_name` demands a
        \\compile-time index, and `#inline while` records its counter as one;
        \\`#inline for` deliberately does not, which is the difference between
        \\the two constructs and is covered in `#inline for`.
    );
    paragraph(23, #string,oneline
        \\Each iteration must change some compile-time variable that the
        \\condition reads. Without that the unroller cannot terminate, so it
        \\refuses rather than looping, and there is a hard cap on the count in
        \\case the change never reaches the bound.
    );
    table_header(24, "Written   —   Diagnostic");
    table_row(25, "a runtime counter   |   `#inline while condition must be compile-time evaluable`");
    table_row(26, "a body that never updates it   |   `#inline while made no compile-time progress; update a compile-time variable used by the condition`");
    table_row(27, "more than 100000 iterations   |   `#inline while exceeded 100000 iterations`");
    note(28, #string,oneline
        \\`break` inside `#inline while` is `error: break outside loop`. The
        \\construct unrolls into a run of ordinary statements, so at the point
        \\the checker reaches the `break` there is no loop to leave. The same
        \\applies to a genuinely unrolled `#inline for`. End the unrolling with
        \\the condition instead.
    );
}

render_section_100 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\`for` iterates. Its header names an iterable and, optionally, the
        \\variables the body reads it through; everything else in the header is a
        \\one-token modifier.
    );
    code_block(1, "ebnf", #string
        \\for [ "<" ] [ ":" expansion ] [ value [ "," index ] ":" ] iterable body
    );
    table_header(2, "Piece   —   Written   —   Effect");
    table_row(3, "reverse   |   `<` right after `for`   |   iterate from the high end");
    table_row(4, "expansion   |   `:name`   |   use that iteration macro");
    table_row(5, "value   |   `v:`   |   name the element variable");
    table_row(6, "index   |   `v, i:`   |   name the index variable too");
    paragraph(7, #string,oneline
        \\The value variable defaults to `it` and the index variable to
        \\`it_index`. Renaming them adds names rather than replacing them: the
        \\compiler prepends `v := it;` and `i := it_index;` to the body, so both
        \\the chosen name and the default are live in the same body. `it_index`
        \\is a `u64` and counts iterations from zero, whatever the element type
        \\is.
    );
    code_block(8, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    for 0..3 print("it=% it_index=%\n", it, it_index);
        \\    for i: 0..3 print("i=%\n", i);
        \\    for value, index: 10..13 print("value=% index=%\n", value, index);
        \\    for n: 0..2 print("both live: n=% it=% it_index=%\n", n, it, it_index);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(9, "text", #string
        \\it=0 it_index=0
        \\it=1 it_index=1
        \\it=2 it_index=2
        \\i=0
        \\i=1
        \\i=2
        \\value=10 index=0
        \\value=11 index=1
        \\value=12 index=2
        \\both live: n=0 it=0 it_index=0
        \\both live: n=1 it=1 it_index=1
    );

    subheading(10, "Ranges");
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\A range in the header is iterated by the compiler directly rather than
        \\through a library. Written inline or stored in a variable, all five
        \\usable shapes work; `..` alone is not one of them, because a bare `..`
        \\is `error: bare '..' needs an index context` and cannot be stored at
        \\all.
    );
    table_header(12, "Shape   —   Written   —   Iterates");
    table_row(13, "`Range`   |   `a..b`   |   `a` up to but not including `b`");
    table_row(14, "`RangeInclusive`   |   `a..=b`   |   `a` through `b`");
    table_row(15, "`RangeTo`   |   `..b`   |   the origin up to `b`");
    table_row(16, "`RangeToInclusive`   |   `..=b`   |   the origin through `b`");
    table_row(17, "`RangeFrom`   |   `a..`   |   `a` upwards, without end");
    code_block(18, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    for i: 0..=3 print("inclusive %\n", i);
        \\    for < i: 0..4 print("reverse %\n", i);
        \\
        \\    r := 0..3;
        \\    for i: r print("stored Range %\n", i);
        \\
        \\    rt := ..4;
        \\    for i: rt print("RangeTo %\n", i);
        \\
        \\    rti := ..=2;
        \\    for i: rti print("RangeToInclusive %\n", i);
        \\
        \\    rf := 10..;
        \\    for i: rf { print("RangeFrom %\n", i); if i == 12 break; }
        \\
        \\    for < i: rt print("reverse RangeTo %\n", i);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(19, "text", #string
        \\inclusive 0
        \\inclusive 1
        \\inclusive 2
        \\inclusive 3
        \\reverse 3
        \\reverse 2
        \\reverse 1
        \\reverse 0
        \\stored Range 0
        \\stored Range 1
        \\stored Range 2
        \\RangeTo 0
        \\RangeTo 1
        \\RangeTo 2
        \\RangeTo 3
        \\RangeToInclusive 0
        \\RangeToInclusive 1
        \\RangeToInclusive 2
        \\RangeFrom 10
        \\RangeFrom 11
        \\RangeFrom 12
        \\reverse RangeTo 3
        \\reverse RangeTo 2
        \\reverse RangeTo 1
        \\reverse RangeTo 0
    );
    paragraph(20, #string,oneline
        \\The induction variable takes the low bound's type, and a literal low
        \\bound is a compile-time number that takes the default `int`. So
        \\`for i: 0..count` counts in `int` however `count` is typed, while
        \\`for i: lo..hi` with `lo` declared `u8` counts in `u8`. A stored range
        \\does not go through that rule at all: `Range(T)` unified both operands
        \\when it was built, so `r := 0..count; for i: r` counts in `count`'s
        \\type.
    );
    note(21, #string,oneline
        \\A narrow or unsigned induction type does not terminate at the ends of
        \\its range. The loop steps one value past the bound to decide it is
        \\done, which `u8` cannot represent: `for < i: lo..hi` over `u8` bounds
        \\`0` and `3` yields `2, 1, 0, 255, 254, ...` and runs forever, and so
        \\does a forward loop that ends at `255`. Letting the low bound win is
        \\the deliberate defence, because a literal low bound then pins the loop
        \\to `int`; a stored range gives that defence up.
    );
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\Reverse iteration of an open-ended range is `error: reverse for cannot
        \\use an open-ended RangeFrom`, since there is no last element to start
        \\from. A float range is `error: for range needs prev/next for f64`: the
        \\element type must be an integer or supply a `Step` instance. An enum
        \\range is accepted by the checker and then rejected by the lowerer with
        \\`error: range step has no typeclass target`, so iterate an integer
        \\range and cast.
    );

    subheading(23, "Arrays, views, and dynamic arrays");
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\A fixed array, a view and a dynamic array all iterate by element, with
        \\`it_index` counting positions. A view produced by slicing iterates the
        \\slice, and its indices start at zero rather than at the slice's offset.
    );
    code_block(25, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Memory";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    xs: [4]int = .[10, 20, 30, 40];
        \\    for x: xs print("fixed x=% at %\n", x, it_index);
        \\    for x, i: xs[1..3] print("view x=% at %\n", x, i);
        \\
        \\    d: [..]int;
        \\    defer array_free(*d);
        \\    array_add(*d, 7);
        \\    array_add(*d, 8);
        \\    for x, i: d print("dyn x=% at %\n", x, i);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(26, "text", #string
        \\fixed x=10 at 0
        \\fixed x=20 at 1
        \\fixed x=30 at 2
        \\fixed x=40 at 3
        \\view x=20 at 0
        \\view x=30 at 1
        \\dyn x=7 at 0
        \\dyn x=8 at 1
    );
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\A tuple is also iterable, and is unrolled rather than looped, because
        \\its elements need not share a type. Writing `#inline for` over one is
        \\the explicit spelling of the same expansion; see `#inline for`. A
        \\`string` is not iterable: the checker accepts `for c: s` and the
        \\lowerer rejects it with `error: for target is not iterable`, since a
        \\string has no element type to step through.
    );
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\Two behaviours of the array loop are worth knowing before relying on
        \\them. The element variable is a copy, so assigning to it does not write
        \\back; and `for <` over an array, a view or a dynamic array does not
        \\reverse anything, because the array lowering ignores the flag. Only
        \\ranges and custom expansions that honour the flag actually run
        \\backwards.
    );
    code_block(29, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    xs: [3]int = .[1, 2, 3];
        \\    for < v: xs print("for < over an array: %\n", v);
        \\
        \\    for v: xs v += 100;
        \\    print("array unchanged: % % %\n", xs[0], xs[1], xs[2]);
        \\
        \\    for i: 0..5 { print("range i=%\n", i); i += 2; }
        \\    for v, k: xs { print("index k=%\n", k); k += 1; }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(30, "text", #string
        \\for < over an array: 1
        \\for < over an array: 2
        \\for < over an array: 3
        \\array unchanged: 1 2 3
        \\range i=0
        \\range i=3
        \\index k=0
        \\index k=2
    );
    note(31, #string,oneline
        \\Assigning to the induction variable of a range loop changes the loop
        \\counter, and so does assigning to the index variable of an array loop.
        \\Only the element variable of an array loop is a copy. The output above
        \\shows all three: the range loop visits `0` and `3`, the array's
        \\elements are untouched, and the array loop visits indices `0` and `2`.
    );

    subheading(32, "Iterating by pointer");
    paragraph(33, #string,oneline
        \\There is no by-pointer `for`. `for *v: xs` and `for * v: xs` are both
        \\`error: unexpected token ':'`. The prelude's `For_Flags` declares a
        \\`POINTER` bit, but the compiler never sets it: the flags argument it
        \\builds is `For_Flags.REVERSE` for a reverse loop and a cast zero
        \\otherwise.
    );
    paragraph(34, #string,oneline
        \\To write through the elements, iterate a range of indices and index the
        \\array. The index is the loop's own variable, so this is also the way to
        \\walk two arrays in step.
    );
    code_block(35, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    xs: [4]int = .[10, 20, 30, 40];
        \\    for i: 0..xs.count xs[i] += 1;
        \\    print("after write-through: % % % %\n", xs[0], xs[1], xs[2], xs[3]);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(36, "text", "after write-through: 11 21 31 41");
    paragraph(37, #string,oneline
        \\`break`, `continue` and `defer` behave in a `for` exactly as they do in
        \\a `while`: `break` leaves the loop, `continue` starts the next
        \\iteration, and a `defer` in the body runs at the end of every
        \\iteration. A target that is neither a range, an array, a tuple nor a
        \\type with an iteration macro is `error: for-each needs an array, got
        \\P`; supplying that macro is Custom Iteration with `for_expansion`.
    );
}

render_section_101 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\A `for` whose target is not a range, an array or a tuple dispatches to
        \\a macro named `for_expansion`, chosen by the target's type. The
        \\contract is a one-method typeclass, so a container joins the loop
        \\syntax by writing an instance.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\// std/Prelude/Primitive_Typeclasses.glo
        \\Iterable :: typeclass (T: type) {
        \\    for_expansion :: (val: T, body: Code, flags: For_Flags) #expand;
        \\}
        \\
        \\// std/Prelude/Primitive_Operators.glo
        \\For_Flags :: enum_flags u32 {
        \\    POINTER :: 16#1;
        \\    REVERSE :: 16#2;
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(2, #string,oneline
        \\`#expand` makes it a macro: the body is cloned into the loop's place
        \\rather than called. The loop body arrives as the `Code` parameter, and
        \\the expansion decides how many times and in what order to splice it.
        \\When the loop renamed its variables, the compiler has already prepended
        \\`v := it;` and `i := it_index;` to that `Code`, which is why an
        \\expansion never sees the chosen names.
    );

    subheading(3, "What an expansion owes the loop");
    bullet(4, #string,oneline
        \\It declares `it` with a leading backtick, holding the current element.
    );
    bullet(5, #string,oneline
        \\It declares `it_index` with a leading backtick, counting iterations
        \\from zero.
    );
    bullet(6, "It writes `#insert body` where one iteration's work belongs.");
    paragraph(7, #string,oneline
        \\The backticks are load-bearing. Macro expansion renames every local a
        \\macro declares, to keep it from colliding with the caller's names; a
        \\leading backtick opts out of that renaming, which is exactly what these
        \\two names need, since the spliced body refers to them. An expansion
        \\that declares a plain `it` compiles until a loop uses it, and then the
        \\body reports `error: unbound 'it'`.
    );
    code_block(8, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Node :: struct { value: s64; next: ?*Node; }
        \\List :: struct { head: ?*Node; }
        \\
        \\List :: instance Iterable {
        \\    for_expansion :: (list: List, body: Code, flags: For_Flags) #expand {
        \\        cursor := list.head;
        \\        step := 0;
        \\        while #pattern .Some(node) = cursor {
        \\            `it := node.*.value;
        \\            `it_index := step;
        \\            cursor = node.*.next;
        \\            step += 1;
        \\            #insert body;
        \\        }
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    c: Node = .{ .value = 30, .next = .None };
        \\    b: Node = .{ .value = 20, .next = .Some(*c) };
        \\    a: Node = .{ .value = 10, .next = .Some(*b) };
        \\    list: List = .{ .head = .Some(*a) };
        \\
        \\    for list print("default names: % at %\n", it, it_index);
        \\
        \\    total := 0;
        \\    for value, index: list { total += value; print("named: % at %\n", value, index); }
        \\    print("total = %\n", total);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(9, "text", #string
        \\default names: 10 at 0
        \\default names: 20 at 1
        \\default names: 30 at 2
        \\named: 10 at 0
        \\named: 20 at 1
        \\named: 30 at 2
        \\total = 60
    );
    paragraph(10, #string,oneline
        \\A linked list is the case that makes the feature necessary: it has no
        \\contiguous storage and no length, so no array or range spelling
        \\describes it, and yet the loop that walks it reads like every other
        \\loop.
    );

    subheading(11, "The flags");
    paragraph(12, #string,oneline
        \\The third parameter carries what the loop header asked for. The
        \\compiler builds it from the header alone: `For_Flags.REVERSE` when the
        \\header wrote `<`, and a cast zero otherwise.
    );
    table_header(13, "Flag   —   Value   —   Set when");
    table_row(14, "`REVERSE`   |   `16#2`   |   the header is `for <`");
    table_row(15, "`POINTER`   |   `16#1`   |   never; the compiler does not set it");
    paragraph(16, #string,oneline
        \\An expansion that ignores `REVERSE` silently iterates forward, which is
        \\what the built-in array loop does. Honouring it is a matter of reading
        \\the bit and choosing a direction; the prelude's own `Range` instance
        \\does exactly this.
    );
    code_block(17, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Ring :: struct { items: [8]s64; start: s64; count: s64; }
        \\
        \\ring_push :: (r: *Ring, value: s64) {
        \\    r.*.items[(r.*.start + r.*.count) % 8] = value;
        \\    r.*.count += 1;
        \\}
        \\
        \\Ring :: instance Iterable {
        \\    for_expansion :: (r: Ring, body: Code, flags: For_Flags) #expand {
        \\        reverse := (flags & For_Flags.REVERSE) != cast(For_Flags)0;
        \\        step := 0;
        \\        while step < r.count {
        \\            offset := ifx reverse r.count - 1 - step else step;
        \\            `it := r.items[(r.start + offset) % 8];
        \\            `it_index := step;
        \\            step += 1;
        \\            #insert body;
        \\        }
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    r: Ring;
        \\    r.start = 6;
        \\    ring_push(*r, 10);
        \\    ring_push(*r, 20);
        \\    ring_push(*r, 30);
        \\
        \\    for r print("% at %\n", it, it_index);
        \\    for < v, i: r print("reverse % at %\n", v, i);
        \\    for v: r { if v == 20 continue; print("kept %\n", v); }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(18, "text", #string
        \\10 at 0
        \\20 at 1
        \\30 at 2
        \\reverse 30 at 0
        \\reverse 20 at 1
        \\reverse 10 at 2
        \\kept 10
        \\kept 30
    );
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\The ring's elements wrap around its storage, so the three values start
        \\at slot six and continue at slot zero. Nothing about that reaches the
        \\loop: the expansion converts a step count into a slot, and the body
        \\sees only `it` and `it_index`.
    );

    subheading(20, "`break` and `continue`");
    paragraph(21, #string,oneline
        \\The body is spliced literally into whatever loop the expansion wrote,
        \\so `break` leaves that loop and `continue` jumps to its condition test.
        \\`break` needs no thought. `continue` does, because it skips everything
        \\the expansion placed after `#insert body`.
    );
    note(22, #string,oneline
        \\An expansion must advance its cursor before `#insert body`, not after,
        \\or `continue` in a loop body never terminates. The `Ring` above
        \\increments `step` first for this reason. The prelude's `Range` instance
        \\does not, which is visible when a range is forced through it:
        \\`for :for_expansion v: 1..5 { if v == 2 continue; print("v=%\n", v); }`
        \\prints `v=1` and then hangs. Ordinary range and array loops are lowered
        \\by the compiler, not by that instance, and handle `continue` correctly.
    );

    subheading(23, "Naming an expansion");
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\`for :name` selects an expansion by name instead of by type. The named
        \\procedure need not be a typeclass method: any `#expand` macro with the
        \\value, `Code` and `For_Flags` parameters will do, which is the way to
        \\give one type several iteration orders.
    );
    code_block(25, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Span :: struct { start: s64; count: s64; }
        \\
        \\stepping :: (s: Span, body: Code, flags: For_Flags) #expand {
        \\    step := 0;
        \\    while step < s.count {
        \\        `it := s.start + step * 2;
        \\        `it_index := step;
        \\        step += 1;
        \\        #insert body;
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    s: Span = .{ .start = 100, .count = 3 };
        \\    for :stepping v, i: s print("stepping % at %\n", v, i);
        \\    for :for_expansion v, i: 1..4 print("explicit builtin % at %\n", v, i);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(26, "text", #string
        \\stepping 100 at 0
        \\stepping 102 at 1
        \\stepping 104 at 2
        \\explicit builtin 1 at 0
        \\explicit builtin 2 at 1
        \\explicit builtin 3 at 2
    );
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\A name that does not resolve is `error: unknown for expansion
        \\'nosuch'`. `:for_expansion` names the ordinary path explicitly, and on
        \\a range that is not a no-op: it routes the loop through the prelude's
        \\`Iterable` instance rather than the compiler's native range lowering.
    );
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\An explicit expansion cannot be combined with `#inline`, which consults
        \\the name only on the non-inline path; and a type with no instance and
        \\no named expansion is `error: for-each needs an array, got P`. The
        \\related range protocols, `Step` and `RangeOrigin`, are described in
        \\Iteration and Range Typeclasses.
    );
}

render_section_102 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\`#inline for` is an ordinary `for` prefixed with the `#inline`
        \\directive. It asks the checker to replace the loop with one copy of
        \\the body per element rather than emit a loop. The request is honoured
        \\for exactly two iterables: a tuple and a fixed-size array. Every
        \\other iterable either compiles to an ordinary runtime loop or is
        \\rejected outright.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\The set is that small because unrolling means the body is checked
        \\once per element, and only a tuple and a fixed array have a length
        \\the checker knows. A view, a dynamic array and a user `Iterable`
        \\all carry their count at run time, so there is no number of copies
        \\to make.
    );
    code_block(2, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    xs :: .[2, 3, 5];
        \\    p := 0;
        \\    #inline for x: xs p += x;
        \\    print("p=%\n", p);
        \\
        \\    t :: .(1, "two", 3.0);
        \\    #inline for t print("tuple elem: %\n", it);
        \\
        \\    q := 0;
        \\    #inline for n: 0..3 q += n;
        \\    print("q=%\n", q);
        \\
        \\    ys: [3]s64 = .[10, 20, 30];
        \\    #inline for v, i: ys print("v=% i=%\n", v, i);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(3, "text", #string
        \\p=10
        \\tuple elem: 1
        \\tuple elem: two
        \\tuple elem: 3
        \\q=3
        \\v=10 i=0
        \\v=20 i=1
        \\v=30 i=2
    );
    paragraph(4, #string,oneline
        \\Three of those four loops behave differently. The tuple and the two
        \\arrays are expanded into straight-line statements; the `0..3` range
        \\loop is a plain runtime loop that happens to carry a directive the
        \\range path never reads. The array case does not require the array's
        \\elements to be constants, only its length: `ys` is an ordinary local
        \\and still unrolls.
    );
    table_header(5, "Iterable   —   Effect of `#inline for`");
    table_row(6, "a tuple literal, `.(a, b, c)`   |   unrolled; `#inline` is optional, a tuple is always expanded statically");
    table_row(7, "a fixed array, `[N]T`   |   unrolled into N copies; this is what `#inline` buys");
    table_row(8, "a literal range, `0..n` or `0..=n`   |   an ordinary runtime loop; the directive is ignored");
    table_row(9, "a stored `Range` value   |   rejected");
    table_row(10, "a view `[]T` or a dynamic array `[..]T`   |   rejected");
    table_row(11, "a type with an `Iterable` instance   |   rejected");
    paragraph(12, #string,oneline
        \\The two rejections have distinct wording. A view or a dynamic array
        \\gives `error: #inline for needs a compile-time iterable; a
        \\runtime-length array expansion cannot be unrolled`, because the type
        \\is an array and only the length is missing. Everything else gives
        \\`error: #inline for expects a compile-time array`, which is what a
        \\stored range, an `Iterable` struct, and a bare integer all produce.
        \\Putting `#inline` in front of any other statement is `error: #inline
        \\can only prefix while or for`.
    );
    subheading(13, "Heterogeneous elements");
    paragraph(14, #string,oneline
        \\Unrolling is what makes a tuple iterable at all. Each copy of the
        \\body is checked against the type of the element it was made for, so
        \\one loop can call a generic procedure at four different
        \\instantiations and can branch on the element type with `#if`.
    );
    code_block(15, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Meta";
        \\
        \\describe :: (v: $T) {
        \\    print("% : %\n", v, type_name(T));
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    row :: .(7, "seven", 7.5, true);
        \\    #inline for row describe(it);
        \\
        \\    #inline for row {
        \\        #if type_of(it) == string print("  a string\n");
        \\        else                      print("  not a string\n");
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(16, "text", #string
        \\7 : int
        \\seven : string
        \\7.5 : f64
        \\1 : bool
        \\  not a string
        \\  a string
        \\  not a string
        \\  not a string
    );
    paragraph(17, #string,oneline
        \\Writing `for row` without the directive produces the same expansion.
        \\A tuple has no runtime element type to loop over, so the checker
        \\takes the static path whether or not `#inline` is present. The
        \\directive matters only for a fixed array, where both lowerings exist
        \\and the plain `for` would emit a real loop.
    );
    subheading(18, "The loop variable is not a compile-time constant");
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\Each unrolled copy begins with a synthesised `it := <element>` and an
        \\`it_index := <literal>`, declared as ordinary non-constant locals.
        \\`it` therefore has a known type in every copy but no known value, and
        \\any construct that demands a compile-time value fails.
    );
    code_block(20, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Format";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    xs :: .[1, 2, 3];
        \\    #inline for xs {
        \\        #insert tprint("k_% :: %;", it, it);
        \\    }
        \\    print("%\n", k_1);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(21, "text", "error: unknown const it");
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\`#inline while` does record its counter. The two constructs unroll
        \\through different code paths, and only the `while` path raises the
        \\checker's inline-expansion depth, which is the flag that turns an
        \\assignment into a recorded compile-time value.
    );
    code_block(23, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Format";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    i ::= 0;
        \\    #inline while i < 3 {
        \\        #insert tprint("k_% :: % * 10;", i, i);
        \\        i += 1;
        \\    }
        \\    print("% % %\n", k_0, k_1, k_2);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(24, "text", "0 10 20");
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\The difference is deliberate and the checker records why at that code
        \\path: raising the depth in the `for` path does make `it` a known
        \\constant, but it also changes constant folding and const-argument
        \\resolution inside the body, which moves conformance cases. It is a
        \\change to make on its own rather than a side effect of unrolling.
    );
    paragraph(26, #string,oneline
        \\The most common way to hit the rule is an array of types. `type_name`
        \\and `size_of` take their argument as a compile-time `type`, so the
        \\loop variable cannot be handed to them.
    );
    code_block(27, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Meta";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    Ts :: .[s64, f64, bool, string];
        \\    #inline for T: Ts {
        \\        print("% is % bytes\n", type_name(T), size_of(T));
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(28, "text", "error: unknown const T");
    paragraph(29, #string,oneline
        \\The replacement is an `#inline while` over a compile-time index,
        \\which indexes the same constant array. The index is a recorded
        \\constant, so `Ts[i]` folds and the intrinsic accepts it.
    );
    code_block(30, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Meta";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    Ts :: .[s64, f64, bool];
        \\    i ::= 0;
        \\    #inline while i < 3 {
        \\        print("%\n", type_name(Ts[i]));
        \\        i += 1;
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(31, "text", #string
        \\int
        \\f64
        \\bool
    );
    note(32, #string,oneline
        \\The restriction is on the loop variable's value, not its type. A
        \\generic call binds `$T` from the argument's type and works
        \\per element, and `#if type_of(it) == string` selects a branch per
        \\copy. Only intrinsics and declarations that need the value itself
        \\are affected.
    );
    subheading(33, "break, scope, and defer");
    paragraph(34, #string,oneline
        \\A genuinely unrolled `#inline for` produces a list of blocks, not a
        \\loop, so there is nothing for `break` or `continue` to leave.
    );
    code_block(35, "glosso", #string
        \\xs :: .[1, 2, 3, 4];
        \\total := 0;
        \\#inline for x: xs { if x == 3 break; total += x; }
    );
    code_block(36, "text", "error: break outside loop");
    paragraph(37, #string,oneline
        \\The same body is accepted under `#inline for n: 0..6`, because that
        \\form was never unrolled and is a real loop with a real exit edge.
        \\This is the clearest test of whether a given `#inline for` unrolled:
        \\if `break` compiles, it did not.
    );
    paragraph(38, #string,oneline
        \\Each copy is wrapped in its own block, so a local declared in the
        \\body does not collide across copies and a `defer` in the body runs at
        \\the end of the copy that declared it rather than at the end of the
        \\whole expansion.
    );
    code_block(39, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    xs :: .[1, 2, 3];
        \\    #inline for xs {
        \\        doubled := it * 2;
        \\        defer print("leaving %\n", it);
        \\        print("doubled %\n", doubled);
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(40, "text", #string
        \\doubled 2
        \\leaving 1
        \\doubled 4
        \\leaving 2
        \\doubled 6
        \\leaving 3
    );
    bullet(41, #string,oneline
        \\Assigning to the element variable writes to that copy's local only.
        \\`#inline for v: xs { v += 100; }` leaves `xs` unchanged, exactly as
        \\the runtime array loop does.
    );
    subheading(42, "Reverse, and explicit expansions");
    paragraph(43, #string,oneline
        \\`#inline for <` reverses. This is worth stating on its own because
        \\the runtime array loop does not: `for < v: xs` over an array, a view
        \\or a dynamic array silently iterates forward, while the unroller
        \\emits its copies in reverse index order.
    );
    code_block(44, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    xs: [3]s64 = .[1, 2, 3];
        \\    print("inline reverse:\n");
        \\    #inline for < v, i: xs print("  v=% i=%\n", v, i);
        \\    print("runtime reverse:\n");
        \\    for < v, i: xs print("  v=% i=%\n", v, i);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(45, "text", #string
        \\inline reverse:
        \\  v=3 i=2
        \\  v=2 i=1
        \\  v=1 i=0
        \\runtime reverse:
        \\  v=1 i=0
        \\  v=2 i=1
        \\  v=3 i=2
    );
    paragraph(46, #string,oneline
        \\An explicit expansion selector cannot be combined with `#inline`. The
        \\checker consults the `for_expansion` name only on the non-inline
        \\path, so `#inline for :looping v: d` skips the macro, falls through
        \\to the static path, and reports `error: #inline for expects a
        \\compile-time array`. Custom iteration is covered in Custom Iteration
        \\with `for_expansion`, and the unrolled `while` in `while` and
        \\`#inline while`.
    );
    paragraph(47, #string,oneline
        \\One further position accepts `#inline for`: the case list of a
        \\`#code` quote, where it expands into real switch arms instead of
        \\statements. That is the mechanism the derive macros use, and it is
        \\described in Writing a Derive Macro.
    );
}

render_section_103 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\A label marks a position in a procedure body. It is written as a
        \\single leading quote followed by an identifier, `'name`, and it is
        \\the destination of a `goto`. Neither the label nor `goto` is a
        \\keyword: the label is a token kind the lexer produces, and `goto` is
        \\a procedure in `std/Jump.glo`.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\The lexer decides between a label and an operator name by looking for
        \\the closing quote. After `'` it takes an identifier run, and if the
        \\character following that run is not another `'` the token is a label.
        \\So `'add` is a label and `'add'` is an operator name; the two never
        \\collide. Operator names are covered in Reserved Operators and
        \\Operator Names.
    );
    paragraph(2, #string,oneline
        \\A label statement is written `'name;`. The terminator is
        \\load-bearing: the parser reads a label statement only when the token
        \\after the label is a terminator, so the semicolon is what separates
        \\defining a label from mentioning one.
    );
    code_block(3, "glosso", #string
        \\'first;      // label statement: defines the label
        \\'third       // NOT a label statement - an expression of type `label`
    );
    paragraph(4, #string,oneline
        \\A bare `'third` on its own line is an expression statement whose
        \\value is a label literal, so no label is defined and a later
        \\`goto('third)` reports that the procedure has no such label. A label
        \\statement at file scope is not a statement at all and is rejected as
        \\a declaration: `error: bad decl name 'here'`.
    );
    note(5, #string,oneline
        \\The parser also accepts a colon, `'name:`, and defines the same
        \\label. Nothing in the standard library or the examples uses it, and
        \\this manual does not, so treat `'name;` as the spelling. The colon
        \\form survives in one visible place: the compiler's own suggestion
        \\when a jump names a label the procedure does not declare reads
        \\`error: no label 'nowhere' in this procedure (define it with
        \\`'nowhere:`)`. Write the semicolon anyway.
    );
    subheading(6, "Jumping");
    paragraph(7, #string,oneline
        \\`goto` requires `#import "Jump";`. Without it the call is `error:
        \\unknown function goto`. The declaration takes the target and three
        \\optional parameters that only the context-restoring modes use; the
        \\one-argument form is the plain jump and keeps the compiler's native
        \\local and computed-label lowering.
    );
    code_block(8, "glosso", #string
        \\goto :: (
        \\    target: label,
        \\    ctx: ?*Execution_Context = .None,
        \\    value: any = false,
        \\    return_code: s32 = 1,
        \\) #noreturn #magic "jump_goto"
        \\  #memory unknown;
        \\
        \\label_is_valid :: (target: label) -> bool #magic "label_is_valid";
        \\label_is_local :: (target: label) -> bool #magic "label_is_local";
    );
    code_block(9, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Jump";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    i := 0;
        \\'top;
        \\    i += 1;
        \\    if i < 3 goto('top);
        \\
        \\    goto('done);
        \\    print("skipped\n");
        \\'done;
        \\    print("i=%\n", i);
        \\
        \\    target := 'first;
        \\    target = 'second;
        \\    goto(target);
        \\'first;
        \\    print("wrong\n");
        \\'second;
        \\    print("right\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(10, "text", #string
        \\i=3
        \\right
    );
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\`goto` is `#noreturn`, which is why `print("skipped\n")` above is
        \\never reached and never lowered. Its `#memory unknown` contract tells
        \\the memory analysis that a transfer may do anything to reachable
        \\storage, which is the honest description of a jump that can land in
        \\another frame.
    );
    subheading(12, "The label pre-pass");
    paragraph(13, #string,oneline
        \\Before a procedure body is checked, a pre-pass walks the whole body
        \\and collects every label it finds, recursing into loop bodies, `then`
        \\and `else` branches, switch case bodies and bare blocks. Labels are
        \\therefore function-scoped, not block-scoped, and a `goto` may name a
        \\label that has not been written yet or that sits deeper than the
        \\jump. A name the pre-pass never saw is rejected.
    );
    code_block(14, "text", #string
        \\error: no label 'nowhere' in this procedure (define it with `'nowhere:`)
    );
    paragraph(15, #string,oneline
        \\The outward jump is the everyday use. `break` and `continue` take no
        \\label, so leaving a nested loop from the inside is what `goto` is
        \\for.
    );
    code_block(16, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Jump";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    for i: 0..4 {
        \\        for j: 0..4 {
        \\            if i * j > 4 goto('escaped);
        \\            print("i=% j=%\n", i, j);
        \\        }
        \\    }
        \\'escaped;
        \\    print("escaped\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(17, "text", #string
        \\i=0 j=0
        \\i=0 j=1
        \\i=0 j=2
        \\i=0 j=3
        \\i=1 j=0
        \\i=1 j=1
        \\i=1 j=2
        \\i=1 j=3
        \\i=2 j=0
        \\i=2 j=1
        \\i=2 j=2
        \\escaped
    );
    paragraph(18, #string,oneline
        \\An inward jump works as well, in both directions. Execution resumes
        \\at the label; the block's own entry code, including any
        \\initialisation written above the label, does not run.
    );
    code_block(19, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Jump";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    n := 1;
        \\    if n == 1 goto('inside);
        \\    print("not skipped by noreturn\n");
        \\    {
        \\        print("block head\n");
        \\'inside;
        \\        print("block tail\n");
        \\    }
        \\    print("after block\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(20, "text", #string
        \\block tail
        \\after block
    );
    note(21, #string,oneline
        \\A forward `goto` into a nested block does not compile when the jump
        \\is unconditional. Because `goto` is `#noreturn`, everything after it is
        \\discarded up to the next label in the same statement list, and a
        \\label buried inside a block does not stop that scan. The block is
        \\dropped whole, and lowering then cannot find the destination:
        \\`error: branch targets unknown label 'U.inside' [opcode Jmp] while
        \\lowering 'main' at IR instruction 0`. Guarding the jump with an `if`,
        \\as above, or placing the target block earlier in the body, both
        \\avoid it.
    );
    subheading(22, "Duplicate labels");
    paragraph(23, #string,oneline
        \\A label may be defined once per procedure. Two procedures in the same
        \\file may each define `'entry`; two `'entry` statements in one
        \\procedure may not.
    );
    code_block(24, "text", "error: function 'main' defines label 'U.again' more than once");
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\This is also how a labelled `break` fails. Glosso has no labelled
        \\`break` or `continue`; `break 'outer;` parses as a `break` statement
        \\followed by a separate label statement `'outer`, which then collides
        \\with the real `'outer` the program declared and produces the
        \\duplicate-label error rather than a syntax error.
    );
    subheading(26, "`label` is a type");
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\A label in value position is an expression of the built-in type
        \\`label`. It can be stored in a variable, passed to a procedure, held
        \\in a struct field, and compared. `#undefined` is the absent label,
        \\and `label_is_valid` distinguishes it from a real one. A `goto`
        \\through a variable is a computed jump and is not checked against the
        \\procedure's label set.
    );
    table_header(28, "Form   —   Meaning");
    table_row(29, "`'name;`   |   statement; defines the label");
    table_row(30, "`'name`   |   expression of type `label`");
    table_row(31, "`#undefined`   |   the absent label; `label_is_valid` is false");
    table_row(32, "`proc,,'name`   |   a stable entry point at `'name` inside `proc`");
    table_row(33, "`label_is_local(t)`   |   true only for a label in the procedure making the call");
    code_block(34, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Jump";
        \\
        \\Handler :: struct { landing: label; name: string; }
        \\
        \\first :: () {
        \\    n := 0;
        \\'entry;
        \\    n += 1;
        \\    if n < 2 goto('entry);
        \\    print("first n=%\n", n);
        \\}
        \\
        \\second :: () {
        \\    m := 0;
        \\'entry;
        \\    m += 2;
        \\    if m < 4 goto('entry);
        \\    print("second m=%\n", m);
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    first();
        \\    second();
        \\    h := Handler.{ .landing = 'stop, .name = "stop" };
        \\    print("field valid: % name %\n", label_is_valid(h.landing), h.name);
        \\    absent: label = #undefined;
        \\    print("absent is label: %\n", label_is_valid(absent));
        \\    goto(h.landing);
        \\    print("skipped\n");
        \\'stop;
        \\    print("stopped\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(35, "text", #string
        \\first n=2
        \\second m=4
        \\field valid: 1 name stop
        \\absent is label: 0
        \\stopped
    );
    paragraph(36, #string,oneline
        \\The `,,` operator builds a label value naming a position in another
        \\procedure. Such a target is valid but not local, and entering it
        \\bypasses the ordinary call sequence, so arguments have to travel
        \\another way. That is what the payload mechanism in Explicit Control
        \\Transfer with the Jump Library is for.
    );
    subheading(37, "`goto` and `defer`");
    paragraph(38, #string,oneline
        \\A `goto` does not run pending `defer` statements. `defer` is tied to
        \\block exit, and a transfer is not a block exit: it abandons the
        \\block without unwinding it. This is the one behaviour that separates
        \\`goto` from `break`, `continue` and `return`, all of which do run
        \\their defers.
    );
    code_block(39, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Jump";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    {
        \\        defer print("inner defer\n");
        \\        print("before goto\n");
        \\        goto('out);
        \\    }
        \\'out;
        \\    print("after goto\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(40, "text", #string
        \\before goto
        \\after goto
    );
    note(41, #string,oneline
        \\Any release obligation parked in a `defer` is skipped by a jump over
        \\it. Free the resource before the `goto`, or place the obligation in a
        \\scope the jump does not leave. What the compiler tracks here is
        \\described in Ownership and Release Obligations.
    );
    subheading(42, "Targets");
    paragraph(43, #string,oneline
        \\A `goto` whose target is a label literal in the same procedure lowers
        \\to a plain branch and works on every target the compiler emits,
        \\WebAssembly included. A computed target, whether a stored `label` or
        \\a `,,` entry point, needs an indirect jump instruction and is
        \\rejected where the backend has none: `error: raw label goto is
        \\unsupported on portable WASM targets`. Other CPUs report `raw label
        \\goto is unsupported for target CPU '<cpu>'`.
    );
}

render_section_104 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\`std/Jump.glo` builds setjmp and longjmp, exceptions, stackful
        \\coroutines, awaitables and a scheduler on a single transfer
        \\operation. That operation is `goto`. Everything else in the module is
        \\ordinary Glosso written on top of it, which is why the library has
        \\one primitive and no privileged internal entry point.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\A `label` is only a code address; it carries no machine state. The
        \\state lives in `Execution_Context`, which owns a saved register image,
        \\a resume address, an inbox for the value a transfer carries, and a
        \\pointer to the logical execution-local state that holds the exception
        \\stack, the current coroutine and the jump payload.
    );
    code_block(2, "glosso", #string
        \\Context_Storage :: struct #magic "jump_context_storage_struct" {}
        \\
        \\Execution_Context :: struct {
        \\    machine: Context_Storage;
        \\    resume_at: label;
        \\    inbox: any;
        \\    local: ?*Execution_Local;
        \\}
        \\
        \\Execution_Local :: struct {
        \\    handler_top: ?*Exception_Frame;
        \\    caught: ?*Exception_Frame;
        \\    coroutine: ?*Coroutine;
        \\    scheduler: ?*Scheduler;
        \\    jump_payload: [..]any;
        \\    pending_jump_payload: [..]any;
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\`Context_Storage` is opaque: the compiler supplies its size and
        \\alignment for the target. On `x86_64-pc-windows-msvc`,
        \\`size_of(Context_Storage)` is 640. A program never reads its fields;
        \\it only ever hands the address of the enclosing `Execution_Context`
        \\to `context_save`, `context_prepare` and `goto`.
    );
    subheading(4, "`context_save`");
    paragraph(5, #string,oneline
        \\`context_save` captures the current continuation into a context and
        \\returns `0`. When that context is later restored, the same call
        \\returns again, this time with the restoring `goto`'s `return_code`,
        \\and the `goto`'s `value` has been written into `ctx.inbox`. The
        \\declaration carries `#returns_twice` so the backend keeps the frame
        \\in a shape that survives the second return.
    );
    code_block(6, "glosso", #string
        \\context_save :: (ctx: *Execution_Context) -> s32
        \\    #returns_twice #magic "jump_context_save"
        \\    #memory(writes(ctx), noescape(ctx));
        \\
        \\context_prepare :: (
        \\    ctx: *Execution_Context,
        \\    stack: []u8,
        \\    local: ?*Execution_Local = .None,
        \\) #magic "jump_context_prepare"
        \\  #memory(writes(ctx), escapes(stack), escapes(local));
    );
    code_block(7, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Jump";
        \\
        \\resume_saved :: (saved: *Execution_Context) #noreturn {
        \\    goto(#undefined, .ctx = saved, .value = "carried", .return_code = 7);
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    saved: Execution_Context;
        \\    saved.local = execution_local();
        \\
        \\    code := context_save(*saved);
        \\    if code == 0 {
        \\        print("captured, code=%\n", code);
        \\        resume_saved(*saved);
        \\    }
        \\    print("restored, code=% inbox=%\n", code, saved.inbox);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(8, "text", #string
        \\captured, code=0
        \\restored, code=7 inbox=carried
    );
    paragraph(9, #string,oneline
        \\Setting `saved.local = execution_local()` before the capture is not
        \\optional bookkeeping. A restored context becomes the active execution
        \\local, so a context captured without one would resume with no
        \\exception stack and no payload storage.
    );
    subheading(10, "The three modes of `goto`");
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\`goto`'s optional `ctx` parameter is what separates a jump from a
        \\context switch, and its `target` selects where the switched-to
        \\context resumes. The three combinations are the whole interface.
    );
    table_header(12, "Target   —   `ctx`   —   Effect");
    table_row(13, "a valid label   |   absent   |   raw transfer to that label in the current context");
    table_row(14, "`#undefined`   |   present   |   restore the context and resume where it was saved");
    table_row(15, "a valid label   |   present   |   restore the context, overriding its resume address with the label");
    paragraph(16, #string,oneline
        \\`value` and `return_code` are only meaningful in the two restoring
        \\modes. `value` is stored in the destination context's `inbox` before
        \\control arrives, and `return_code` becomes the value the destination's
        \\`context_save` returns; it defaults to `1` so a restored capture can
        \\never be mistaken for the original `0`.
    );
    subheading(17, "`context_prepare`");
    paragraph(18, #string,oneline
        \\`context_prepare` builds a complete machine image over a stack the
        \\caller owns, without transferring control. Entering it with the
        \\third mode above runs the named label on that stack. This is the
        \\primitive behind coroutines.
    );
    code_block(19, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Jump";
        \\
        \\State :: struct {
        \\    fresh: Execution_Context;
        \\    fresh_local: Execution_Local;
        \\    caller: Execution_Context;
        \\    stack: [..]u8;
        \\}
        \\
        \\#thread_local storage: State = .{};
        \\
        \\on_fresh_stack :: () #noreturn {
        \\'entry;
        \\    state := *storage;
        \\    print("running on the fresh stack, inbox=%\n", state.*.fresh.inbox);
        \\    goto(#undefined, .ctx = *state.*.caller, .value = "and back", .return_code = 9);
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    state := *storage;
        \\    stack: [..]u8;
        \\    for i: 0..64 * 1024 array_add(*stack, cast(u8)0);
        \\    state.*.stack = stack;
        \\
        \\    context_prepare(*state.*.fresh, array_view(state.*.stack), .local = *state.*.fresh_local);
        \\    state.*.caller.local = execution_local();
        \\
        \\    code := context_save(*state.*.caller);
        \\    if code == 0 {
        \\        goto(on_fresh_stack,,'entry, .ctx = *state.*.fresh, .value = "hello");
        \\    }
        \\    print("caller resumed: code=% inbox=%\n", code, state.*.caller.inbox);
        \\    array_free(*state.*.stack);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(20, "text", #string
        \\running on the fresh stack, inbox=hello
        \\caller resumed: code=9 inbox=and back
    );
    note(21, #string,oneline
        \\The context, its `Execution_Local` and the backing stack must all
        \\outlive every transfer that can reach them. A label entered
        \\nonlocally has no call arguments and no live caller frame to borrow
        \\from, which is why the example keeps all four in one thread-local
        \\object rather than in `main`'s locals.
    );
    subheading(22, "setjmp and longjmp");
    paragraph(23, #string,oneline
        \\The C pair is two aliases and one four-line procedure:
        \\`Jump_Buffer :: Execution_Context;`, `setjmp :: context_save;`, and
        \\`longjmp(buffer, value)` is `goto(#undefined, .ctx = buffer,
        \\.return_code = value)` with a zero value promoted to `1`.
    );
    code_block(24, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Jump";
        \\#import "Meta";
        \\
        \\buffer: Jump_Buffer;
        \\
        \\deep :: (depth: s32) {
        \\    if depth == 0 longjmp(*buffer, 42);
        \\    deep(depth - 1);
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("Context_Storage is % bytes\n", size_of(Context_Storage));
        \\    buffer.local = execution_local();
        \\    code := setjmp(*buffer);
        \\    if code == 0 {
        \\        print("captured\n");
        \\        deep(3);
        \\    }
        \\    print("unwound with code %\n", code);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(25, "text", #string
        \\Context_Storage is 640 bytes
        \\captured
        \\unwound with code 42
    );
    subheading(26, "Exceptions");
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\`try` is an `#expand` macro taking a `label` and two `#code` blocks.
        \\It expands into the caller, pushes an `Exception_Frame`, captures a
        \\landing context, and assigns the frame's unique label to the `label`
        \\variable passed in. `throw(target, payload)` walks the active frames
        \\for the one carrying that label, so an inner body may select an outer
        \\handler by name; `throw_current` takes the innermost frame instead.
        \\`catch()` reads the payload.
    );
    code_block(28, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Jump";
        \\
        \\divide :: (handler: label, a: int, b: int) -> int {
        \\    if b == 0 throw(handler, "division by zero");
        \\    return a / b;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    outer: label = #undefined;
        \\    inner: label = #undefined;
        \\
        \\    try(outer, #code {
        \\        print("first result %\n", divide(outer, 40, 5));
        \\        try(inner, #code {
        \\            print("before failing call\n");
        \\            divide(outer, 10, 0);
        \\            print("unreachable\n");
        \\        }, #code {
        \\            print("inner caught %\n", catch());
        \\        });
        \\        print("outer body done\n");
        \\    }, #code {
        \\        print("outer caught %\n", catch());
        \\    });
        \\
        \\    print("after try\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(29, "text", #string
        \\first result 8
        \\before failing call
        \\outer caught division by zero
        \\after try
    );
    paragraph(30, #string,oneline
        \\The inner `try` is skipped entirely: the failing call names `outer`,
        \\so the frame walk passes over `inner` and lands in the outer handler,
        \\and neither `outer body done` nor the inner handler runs. For code
        \\that cannot be expanded inline, `try_call(body, handler, data)` takes
        \\two function pointers instead of two quotes and always uses the
        \\innermost frame, and `set_unhandled_exception_handler` installs a
        \\last resort that a `throw_current` with no active frame reaches
        \\instead of terminating the process.
    );
    subheading(31, "Coroutines");
    paragraph(32, #string,oneline
        \\A coroutine is a `context_prepare`d context with an owned stack, its
        \\own `Execution_Local`, and a pointer back to the caller's context.
        \\`coroutine_resume` saves the caller and switches in; `co_yield` saves
        \\the coroutine and switches back, carrying a value each way through
        \\the inbox. Because the coroutine has a real stack, it may yield from
        \\any depth.
    );
    table_header(33, "Call   —   Result");
    table_row(34, "`coroutine_create(body, data, stack_size, scheduler)`   |   `?*Coroutine`; absent when allocation fails");
    table_row(35, "`coroutine_resume(co, input)`   |   runs until the next suspension, returns a `Coroutine_Event`");
    table_row(36, "`co_yield(value)`   |   suspends; returns the next `input`");
    table_row(37, "`co_return(value)`   |   `#noreturn`; ends the coroutine with `Returned`");
    table_row(38, "`coroutine_destroy(co)`   |   frees the stack, the payload arrays and the record");
    paragraph(39, #string,oneline
        \\`Coroutine_Event` is a tagged union of `Yielded`, `Awaiting`,
        \\`Returned` and `Failed`, and `Coroutine_Status` runs `Fresh`,
        \\`Running`, `Suspended`, `Waiting`, `Done`, `Failed`. Resuming a
        \\coroutine that is already running, done or failed does not return an
        \\error: it exits the process with status 1.
    );
    code_block(40, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Jump";
        \\
        \\counter :: (data: ?*void) -> any {
        \\    input := co_yield(10);
        \\    print("coroutine received %\n", input);
        \\    input = co_yield(20);
        \\    print("coroutine received %\n", input);
        \\    return 30;
        \\}
        \\
        \\report :: (event: Coroutine_Event) {
        \\    if event == {
        \\    case .Yielded(value);  print("yielded %\n", value);
        \\    case .Awaiting;        print("awaiting\n");
        \\    case .Returned(value); print("returned %\n", value);
        \\    case .Failed(error);   print("failed %\n", error);
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    if #pattern .Some(co) = coroutine_create(counter) {
        \\        report(coroutine_resume(co, 0));
        \\        report(coroutine_resume(co, 100));
        \\        report(coroutine_resume(co, 200));
        \\        print("status done: %\n", co.*.status == .Done);
        \\        coroutine_destroy(co);
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(41, "text", #string
        \\yielded 10
        \\coroutine received 100
        \\yielded 20
        \\coroutine received 200
        \\returned 30
        \\status done: 1
    );
    paragraph(42, #string,oneline
        \\The coroutine body never sees `goto`. It returns a value like any
        \\procedure, and an internal trampoline wraps it in a `try_call` so a
        \\throw that escapes the body arrives at the caller as a `Failed`
        \\event rather than unwinding past a stack that no longer has a caller.
    );
    subheading(43, "Awaitables and the scheduler");
    paragraph(44, #string,oneline
        \\`co_await` suspends into the `Waiting` status and links the coroutine
        \\onto the awaitable's waiter list. `awaitable_complete` publishes the
        \\result and enqueues every waiter on the scheduler each one was
        \\created with. `scheduler_run` drains the ready queue, re-enqueuing
        \\anything that merely yielded and leaving anything that is awaiting.
    );
    code_block(45, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Jump";
        \\
        \\waiter :: (data: ?*void) -> any {
        \\    if #pattern .Some(p) = data {
        \\        value := co_await(cast(*Awaitable)p);
        \\        print("ready=%\n", value);
        \\        return 9;
        \\    }
        \\    return 0;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    scheduler: Scheduler;
        \\    awaitable: Awaitable;
        \\    awaitable_init(*awaitable);
        \\
        \\    created := coroutine_create(waiter, cast(*void)*awaitable, .scheduler = *scheduler);
        \\    if #pattern .Some(co) = created {
        \\        scheduler_enqueue(*scheduler, co);
        \\        scheduler_run(*scheduler);
        \\        print("waiting=%\n", co.*.status == .Waiting);
        \\        awaitable_complete(*awaitable, 7);
        \\        scheduler_run(*scheduler);
        \\        print("done=%\n", co.*.status == .Done);
        \\        coroutine_destroy(co);
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(46, "text", #string
        \\waiting=1
        \\ready=7
        \\done=1
    );
    subheading(47, "Nonlocal entry and the payload");
    paragraph(48, #string,oneline
        \\Entering a `,,` label bypasses the calling sequence, so arguments
        \\cannot ride in registers. `long_goto` is an `#expand` macro that
        \\handles both cases: when `label_is_local` says the target is in the
        \\same procedure it jumps without evaluating its arguments at all,
        \\otherwise it packs the variadic pack into the execution-local payload
        \\and then jumps. The destination reads it with `payload()` or the
        \\typed `payload_get(T, i)` and calls `clear_payload()`.
    );
    code_block(49, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Jump";
        \\
        \\worker :: () #noreturn {
        \\'entry;
        \\    arguments := payload();
        \\    print("arrived with % arguments\n", arguments.count);
        \\    n := payload_get(int, 0);
        \\    flag := payload_get(bool, 1);
        \\    print("n=% flag=%\n", option_unwrap_or(n, 0), option_unwrap_or(flag, false));
        \\    clear_payload();
        \\    long_goto(main,,'back, "returning");
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    entry := worker,,'entry;
        \\    print("local: % valid: %\n", label_is_local(entry), label_is_valid(entry));
        \\    long_goto(entry, 40, true);
        \\
        \\'back;
        \\    message := payload_get(string, 0);
        \\    print("back with %\n", option_unwrap_or(message, ""));
        \\    clear_payload();
        \\    exit(0);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(50, "text", #string
        \\local: 0 valid: 1
        \\arrived with 2 arguments
        \\n=40 flag=1
        \\back with returning
    );
    paragraph(51, #string,oneline
        \\`payload_get` checks the recorded type name before it reads, so a
        \\mismatched index or type yields `.None` rather than a
        \\reinterpretation. A procedure entered this way has no caller to
        \\return to, which is why `worker` is `#noreturn` and why `main` ends
        \\with `exit(0)` instead of running off the end of a frame that was
        \\never entered normally.
    );
    note(52, #string,oneline
        \\Restoring a context is the one part of the library that is not
        \\available everywhere. A plain local `goto` lowers on every target; a
        \\computed `goto` needs an indirect jump; a `context_save` restore
        \\needs a hand-written register switch, and on a CPU without one the
        \\build stops with `error: context restore is not yet supported for
        \\this target CPU`. Portable programs guard the whole region with
        \\`#if OUT.cpu != Target_CPU.X86_64`, which is what the examples in
        \\`examples/goto.glo`, `examples/exceptions.glo` and
        \\`examples/coroutine.glo` do.
    );
}
